"What is the Latin root of the word ""curriculum,"" and how does its original meaning explain the phrase ""curriculum vitae""?","Part of the 
challenge of dealing with the so-called populist clamour that surrounds 
us lies in being able to separate the strands with which it is w oven. We 
cannot forget the hardships which beset so many of our communities, 
across both Europe and the United States, and the real socio-economic 
proble ms  which  have  arisen  as  a  result  of  gross  inequalities  across  the 
globe: stagnant wages, the closing of industries, the rise of computeris ed 
technology  and  the  concomitant  loss  of  jobs. These  factors  must  be 
acknowledged and addressed if there is to be any hope in separating the 
miseries attending them from the blaming of the ‘ot her’, which has char-
acterised so much of that ‘clamour’. And, of course, there are the related 
issues pertaining to the realities of refugees seeking safety from the hor-
rors of war as well as the genuine  needs of migrants seeking better lives 
for themselves and their families. The challenges facing us are threefold: 
disengaging  these  strands,  and  acknowledging  the  complexities  that 
appear to bind them together; find ing collective responses to them; and 
striving to retain the separateness of these real threats to peace and sta-
bility from the messy, malign and,  at times, messianic ‘trumpetings’ that 
accompany them in the worst of the media and in the political nonsenses 
of unscrupulous and narcissistic self - seekers. And given these challenges, 
asking what we, as educationalists, can now offer by way of unpicking 
these strands and responding to them with as much  wisdom as we can 
muster. This  chapter  explores  the  potential  for  a  wise  curriculum. The  word 
derives from the Latin currere, and refers to a race, or a course. Hence the 
term curriculum vitae as ‘the course of one’s life’.1 I am hopeful that we 
might find new courses, and reclaim older ones, both within our  institu-
tions of learning, and in informal spaces and in our own lives, so that we 
may  envisage,  and  thence  put  into  practice,  ways  of  bei ng  which  have 
Sophia at their heart. I shall continue by attempting to unpick certain of 
the main strands forging the knots of hate with which communities are 
being beaten and abused. I shall then draw upon, among others, Henderson 
and  Kesson’s  (2004)  Curriculum  Wisdom  by  way  of  articulating  what 
