"According to John's account, what were the students' reasons for being present at his session, and what did they hope to learn?","John told me that the 
students were there for the simple reason of having the register acknowl-
edge their presence. They wanted quick instructions in how to wield plas-
ter and tool in order to fill a hole and cover a wall. He couldn’t fail any of 
them because his job would then be under review. ‘It’s all so wrong,’ he 
told me tearfully. ‘And I hate the thought of what our homes will be like 
in the future.’

There  is  another  side  to  this  coin,  and  that  concerns  the  level  o f 
attention  paid  to  one’s  craft  or  skill. Teacher-training  programmes, 
such as the one I used to deliver, demanded evidence that the tutor had 
‘reflected’ sufficiently on their practice bec ause of the importance of 
knowing one’s area of expertise if one were to pass it on successfully to 
others. In Chap. 2, I noted the nonsense of the SMART targets which 
shaped the learning outcomes and syllabi of programmes such as these. The language of ‘reflection’ was similarly reductive and bore little rela-
tion to the dedicated care and thoughtfulness which John exemplified 
in hi s teaching. In other words, I am urging, as alternative, the kind of 
attentiveness, ‘a patient, respectful nurturing of something into disclo-
sure’ (McGilchrist  2009, p. 152), which I alluded to in the Introduction. Language, as I have argued throughout this text, is key, and a similar 
need  to  pay  attention  to  the  metaphors  we  use  in  our  pedagogic 
end eavours relates to the other elements in Henderson and Kesson’s 
list. I shall return to the concepts of poesis and theoria a little further on in 
this chapter. I shall now explore the concepts of ‘praxis (critical inquiry); 
dialogos (multiperspectival inquiry); phronesis (practical, deliberative wis-
dom); [ and] polis (public moral inquiry)’ (2004, p. 47) by way of rein-
vigorating  our  efforts  as  educators  in  line  with  the  broader  political 
struggles at hand. There is nothing new in this endeavour. It is a challenge 
which also brings back to mind the state of adult education as both field 

