Critiques of Educational Structures and Political Economy

5.3 
The late twentieth century also saw important mobilizations  of critical the-
ories  and  classical  Marxism  as  an  analytical  lens  through  which  to  under-
stand black educational conditions. White scholars like Jonathan Kozol, Jean 
Anyon, and Michelle Fine built from the work of Marxists like Samuel Bowles 
and Herbert Gintis, who argue i n Schooling in Capitalist America that school-
ing within capitalist nation-states necessitated disparate outcomes between 
and within racial groups t o maintain socioeconomic class distinctions.70 Paul 
 Willis’s classic text Learning to Labor, drawing from an ethnographic study of 
a British school , provides important clarity about how schooling is a primary 
mechanism for maintaining class designations, dispelling common notions of 
meritocracy .71 Kozol’s Savage Inequalities, Anyon’s Ghetto Schooling, and Fine’s 
Framing Dropouts provide empirical crystallization of the role of urban school- 
ing in the educational and economic dispossession of black children.72

