While racial segregation, and more importantly, the economic and political 
dispossession it was based upon, was the most prevalent poli tical issue facing 
black educators, there was not a single-minded approach to push for deseg-
regation. In his well-known essay, “Does the Negro Need Separate Schools?”, 
written in 1935, Du Bois argues that,

The Negro needs neither segregated schools n or mixed schools. What he 
needs is Education. What he must remember is that there is no magic, 
either in mixed schools or in segregated schools. A mixed school with 
poor and unsympathetic teachers, with hostile public opinion, and no 
teaching  of  truth  concerning  black  folk,  is  bad. A  segregated  school 
with  ignorant  placeholders,  inadequate  equipment,  poor  salaries,  and 
wretched housing, is equally bad. Other things being equal, the mixed 
school is the broader, more natural basis for the education of all youth. It 
gives wider contacts; it inspires greater self-confidence; and suppresses 
the inferiority complex. But other things seldom are equal, and in that 
case, Sympathy, Knowledge, and the Truth, outweigh all that the mixed 
school can offer.51

Du Bois’s  argument powerfully lays out the debate about desegregation occur-
ring within black communities. The poor physical conditions in segregated 
schools were only matched by the social and academic hostility of desegre-
gated schools. It is always important to note the distinction between racial 
segregation, desegregation, and integration. True integration, or the merging 
of people, curricula, teaching practices, cultures, languages, abilities, etc., has 
never existed, broadly, in US s chools. Certainly, there are examples of inte-
gration in individual cases, but there are no large-scale, state-wide or even 
district-wide examples. What gets conflated with integration is racial desegre-
gation, which is simply the placing of students of various racial backgrounds 
into the school s that had already existed for white students. Desegregation 
requires no change in curricula, teaching philosophies, cultural contexts, or 
any other factors that mediate school life. Today, what are typically referred 

