in this text.7 For our purposes it is important to understand that “public ” can 
mean several things. It is often juxtaposed to the “private” or domestic sphere 
(i.e., your home). Public can be a space that is not protected from some form 
of surveillance by others (i.e., a restaurant, even though they are typically pri-
vately  owned) where people gather. Public is also used to confer some type of 
State-management (e.g., public housing, public assistance, public parks, public 
transportation, public sc hools). It is the latter version of public that is of most 
interest in thinking about black educational history. It is important to note that as blackness was constructed as a racial category, 
outside of the bounds of humanity (i.e. whiteness), there was no distinction 
