"How did African American communities work around the discriminatory public funding system that disproportionately directed property tax revenue to white schools, and what term did they use to describe their own practice of voluntarily contributing land, labor, and money to support their schools?","Effectively, 
taxation for public education became weaponized against black communities. By 1930, African American children in Alabama, then 40% of the school-
age population, received 11% of the school funds, a major downward slide 
from  1890 when they received 44% of the school funds…. In Mississippi 
the African American proportion (60%) of the total school-age popu-
lation received about 20% of the annual state school appropriation  in 
1910. The Black-to-White ratio of per pupil expenditures declined in every 
southern state between 1890 and 1910.11

The  logic  of  public  funding  of  ed ucation  through  the  taxation  of  property 
was supposed to redistribute wealth from property owners to the masses. Yet, 
Anderson shows that the disproportional funding of white schools meant that 
all taxpayers, wealthy and poor, black and white, were mostly fundi ng white 
schools. “Throughout the first half of the twentieth century, discriminatory 
funding  meant  not  only  that  public  taxes  were  diverted  mainly  to  suppo rt 
schools for White children, but that Black schools were largely dependent on 
private philanthropy and what African Americans called ‘double taxat ion,’ the 
practice of paying for their schools through voluntary contributions of land, 
labor, and money.”12 These taxation schemes would continue t o plague eco-
nomically disinvested communities facing intergenerational poverty through-
out the twentieth century. As residential racial segregation continued through 
practices of discriminatory housing covenants and redlining, school funding 
through property tax  was solidified in the 1973 San Antonio Independent School 
District v Rodriguez Supreme Court decision and is still used today to protect 
the dispar ate funding of schools.13

As  Gloria  Ladson-Billings  describes  in  her  groundbreaking  presidential 
address to the American Educational Research  Association where she coined 
the term “education debt,” black communities are owed a historical, economic, 
sociopolitical, and moral debt from the  nation with regard to education.14 Each 
of these elements of debt can be found in the double taxation that Anderson 
describes. Black communities responded to this weaponization of taxation by 
creating alternative funding structures, illustrating what James Levy describes 
in  the previous chapter as Black Pragmatic Consciousness. These alternatives 
came in the form of private philanthropic funding and also community-based 
micro-funding. Washington’s Tuskegee Institute was heavily involved in both 

