education reforms. Teachers  connected  the  utility  of  particular  mathematical  domains  to  specific 
career  and  educational  trajectories. Many  teachers  described  how  the  reforms’ 
emphasis on the extended study of mathematics, particularly algebra, was seriously 
problematic  and  u nnecessary. This  view  was  reflected  in  career,  educational 
pathway,  and  advance  course  choices  articulated  by  teachers  in  response  to 
mathematic s  education  reforms. Specifically,  algebra  was  distinguished  from 
geometry and statistics/probability as a domain that was more abstract and was not 
necessary, reaso nable, or practical for all students to learn. The examples illustrate 
how  the  perceived  roles  of  abstraction  and  the  utility  of  different  domains  are 
important dimensions of domain-s pecific beliefs that affect how teachers respond 
to mathematics education reform. SUMMARY AND DISCUSSION 

