What criteria were used to evaluate nominations submitted for the Each One Teach One peer assistance recognition?,"The following thinking and factors attracted me to the idea of developing academic 
support through the peer assistance concept. Firstly, residences such as Weerona and Burgmann over the last decade presented 
ideal conditions for peer academic assistance because of the numbers  of students 
living together for the purpose of university study. Secondly,  inherent  in  this  is  the  consideration  that  peer  assistance  exists  in 
all university colleges, with or without other forms of aca demic support. In fact, 
without a recognised academic support system in place, such as designated tutors, 
residents would go to each other for academic support. Should we in fact interfere 
in this natural, informal and sometimes personal process? I believe so. Moreover, 
I believe we are entitled to expect a commitment by all residents to the academic 
welfare of their fellows – even if in small ways only to  begin. Otherwise why live in 
an academic community? Thirdly, experienced and higher achieving students are generally willing to offer 
academic assistance in College but do not normally instigate the pr ocess until there 
is a perceived need. This factor became the cornerstone in the way that the Each 
One Teach One programme, as I called it, worked. It rested on the following premiss. The recognition of academic leaders is based on support for others. It is a recognition 
that is in the gift of those who have received. Therefore it is not an award because 
this is not in the gift of the college but simply an acknowledgement of residents’ 
assistance for each other. It simply sets out to recognise what they do anyway – help 
each other with study – with a view to propagating the practice. 119

