"What does the author believe residential college communities should fundamentally aim to do, beyond merely providing housing for students?","Colleges  do  this  best. They  can  structure  for  student  interaction  in  the  best 
possible way: individually, one on one, and cross-culturally. They can build both 
identity (I feel as though I belong) and inclusiveness (I know that I belong) through 
shared purpose and shared learning. A good first step to this is to expect all residents to be part of the giving as well 
as the receiving of the benefits of collegiate life, especially  academic support, and to 
recognise residents for doing so, in their assisting other residents. CONCLUSION

In conclusion, these are the universals of the Collegiate Way that I would like to drop 
on you for your consideration and invite you to f ollow up with further engagement. Firstly, we are in the business of education and not simply student accommodation. We go from the domestic to the classical. Secondly, we have a long history of peer 
tutoring  that  both  arises  from  and  fuels  further  our  collegiate  nature. It  happens 
even if we ignore it. It is natural to us and worth recognising and, without undue 
administrative  interference,  encouraging. Thirdly,  we  share  ancient  and  classical 
roots in learning. It is something of which to be undeniably proud. Fourthly, we 
encourage co-operation rather than competition in a university residential setting, 
preferring recognitions for academic support as muc h as if not more than academic 
excellence. Finally, we are in the business of growing people. We treat students not 
only for who they are but also for whom they may become. REFERENCES

