novel,  high  in  quality  and  appropriate”  (p. 89). A  common  conceptualization  of 
creativity refers to convergent and divergent thinking, with the latter being associated 
with being able to explore  many solutions to a given problem rather than following 
a linear process (like the “scientific method as often taught in schools) (Madden 
et al., 2 013). Psychometrics, cognition, social-personality and other domains have 
all approaches and definitions of creativity, however many of these domain-specif ic 
approaches  have  ignored  the  multiple  influences  underlying  that  which  affords 
creativity (Sternberg, 2003); creativity is a [product] of  a confluence of multiple 
factors. I begin with a definition of creativity as a collective endeavor; “a flow of 
affect between assembled bodies, things, and ideas” (Fox, 2013, p. 6) and that it is in 
the relations that creativity is produced “in the most unpredictable and unexpected 
ways” (p. 8), with the unexpectedness leading to innovation, “the implementation 
of ideas into practice” (Shavinina, 2013, p. xxvii) in the form of new artifac ts. This 
perspective of creativity allows one to focus on the contexts that allow creativity 
to  flourish  rather  than  on  designed  interventions  th at  foster  individual  creativity. According  to  Sternberg  (2003),  “creativity  can  lead  to  new  scientific  findings, 
