our class, then they are disrupting other kids’ learning, and that is not fair.” 
To focus the students’ attention on learning , the teachers in high-capacity 
schools built school-wide behaviour management systems that treated the 
students respectfully, that linked behaviour s to their effects on teaching and 
learning, and that connected consequences to the learning environment. In 
general,  this  element  of  practice  received  collective  attention  in  the  early 
stages of school development, such as when the school was  new, when an 
influx  of  new  students  or  teachers  arrived,  or  when  a  series  of  changes 
occurred in rapid succession. In schools that had been functioning at high 
capacity  for  some  time,  student  behaviour  was  not  a  concern. The  senior 
