learning constituting “engaged and self-critical participation in making the 
world  in  which  we  live”  (p. 18). This  conceptualization  of  knowledge  as 
world-making gives the knowledge stories deep significance: if the stories 
we  tell  ourselves  about  pr ofessional  knowledge  are  implicated  in  the 
construction of a professional world, then we had better think long and hard 
about  what  our  knowl edge  stories  look  like,  where  they  come  from,  and 
whether  they  are  helping  us  to  create  an  educational  world  that  serves 
learning  and builds people. Building  professional  knowledge  is  closely  tied  to  the  construct  of 
organizational learning (Argyris & Schon, 1978) and the invention of the  
learning  organization  (Senge,  1990). Much  has  been  written  on  these 
subjects since Senge’s ground-breaking text, but Gherardi (2006) is worried 
about  the  direction  of  this  dis course. She  argues  that  the  field  of  organi-
zational  learning  has  primarily  been  problem  driven  and  managerially 
focused, and she associates t his discourse with such symbolic meanings as 
“activity, domination, rationality, instrumentality, [and] masculinity” (p. 40). Her concern is that this approach fails to capture the full range of authentic 
human  learning,  it  makes  learning  an  individualistic  and  instr umental 
activity, and it renders knowledge a disinterested, disembodied commodity. To supplement (but not replace) problem-driven cognitive reflexivity, she 
introduces  the  notion  of  mystery-driven  learning. Learning  in  the  face  of 
mystery implies that “not only is learning an activity, it is passivity as well: 
