"According to Mitchell and Sackney, why do many people in the educational world fail to recognize alternatives to the mechanistic approach to schooling, even though this approach can negatively affect both students and teachers?","s in particular ways. The organizational narrative currently 
writing  the  scripts  for  teaching  and  learning  is  rooted  in  a  mechanistic 
worldview  that  is  not   particularly  sympathetic  to  or  accommodating  of 
students’ and teachers’ humanness. With machines as the primary analogue 
for  building  educational  systems,  O’Sullivan  (1999)  argues,  “There  is  no 
intrinsic  relationship  of   parts;  parts  are  held  together  by  an  authoritarian 
hierarchy  and  discrete  horizontal  status  divisions”  (p. 53). Our  original 
analysis of the situation was that 

The  mechanistic  worldview  inscribes  a  complex  set  of  assumptions, 
beliefs,  values,  prac tices,  and  understandings  that  are  embedded  so 
deeply into the collective psyche that many people in the educational 
world simply do not see a ny alternatives. It is not that people intend to 
brutalize students or to demoralize teachers. It is that the implicit and 
explicit structures are so powerful and so compelling that they do not 
afford any way to do otherwise. (Mitchell & Sackney, 2000, p. 129) 

Our  observations  in  schools  over  the  past  decade  have  confirmed  this 
analysis. We continue to see the work in most school, even in many schools 
that  are  said  to  be  professional  learning  communities,  to  be  controlled, 
 managed,  and  inscribed  by  complex  designs  enshrined  in  tightly  codified 
curriculum documents, learning expectations, outcome measures, insti tutional 
rules  and  procedures,  policy  manuals,  and  a  host  of  other  writings  that 
direct  and  contain  school  processes. These  arrangements,  which  capture 
what Tyack and Cuban (1995) call the grammar of schooling, do not leave 
much  room  for  learning  to  be  ener gized  by  the  interests  of  teachers  and 
students  or  to  be  enlivened  by  the  serendipitous  circumstances  of  human 
lives. We  contend  that  the  issue  of  sustainability  shifts  the  discourse  away 
from this grammar of schooling toward an awareness that schools are,  in 
fact and not merely in metaphor, living systems functioning within a deep 
internal  and  external  ecology. This  perspective  requires  educators  to  stop 
trying to manage people or to control learning. It invites them to move past 
a shallow understanding of learning or of community and to think deeply 

