you actually saw 23 children with autism. Doesn’t it just say we’re doing 
something right when you could only spot two of them?” These children 
had  been  embraced  for  who  they  were,  an d  the  appreciation  of  their 
uniqueness  had  freed  them  to  move  beyond  a  disability  and  to  enjoy  the 
same presence and place in the cl assroom as the rest of the students. Their 
classmates  and  teachers  were  richer  for  what  they  learned  from  these 
children. When differences are seen as having both cognitive and affective value, 
conflict takes on a new meaning. Conflict will always be part of the human 
condition,  of  course,  because  things  do  not  always  turn  out  well  when 
people  bump  up  against   each  other. If  we  are  hurt,  angry,  confused,  or 
otherwise unhappy with the outcomes or experiences of our lives, we are 
likely,  in  our  humanness,  to   see  the  different  other  as  the  source  of  our 
discomfort. A  living  systems  perspective  does  not  displace  or  erase 
conflict,  but  it  does  offer  a  pathway  for  reframing  and  reclaiming  it. Elsewhere, we presented our point this way: 

