  Performance accomplishments are described as sources of efficacy information 
that are es pecially influential because it is based on personal mastery experiences. As individuals succeed their mastery expectations are raised as opposed to being 
lowered by repeated failures (Bandura, 1977). Failure’s effect on personal efficacy 
depends partly on timing  and total  pattern  of  experiences in  which the failures 
occur. Vicarious experience also impact people’s expectations. “Seeing others perform 
threatening activities without adverse consequences can generate expectations in 
observers that they too will improve if they  intensify and persist in their efforts” 
(Bandura, 1977, p.197). American Black women too would like the experience 
of  seeing  other  American  Black  women  succeed  in  leadership  efforts  that  are 
threatenin g without adverse consequences. This cannot occur if American Black 
women are not in organizations where other American Black women are successful. Vicarious experiences should not be depended upon, alone, to determine success, 
but  modeling  positive  experiences  does  help  to  increase  the   self-efficacy  of 
individuals. Verbal persuasion is used to motivate people, through suggestion, into “believing 
they can cope successfully with what has overwhelmed them in the pa st” (Bandura, 
1977, p.198). Because verbal persuasions are not grounded in experiential experiences, 
they can be easily destroyed by disconfirming experiences. Verbal persuasions alone 
are not enough to increase self-efficacy; they should be complemented by successful 
personal actions and accomplishments. Emotional arousal often occur as a result of stressful and taxing situations and 
