roles of teacher and learner. Chapter 8 elaborates more fully his understanding of 
dialogue and dialectic in relation to Bakhtin’s ideas and Chapter 10 draws on his 
approach to t eaching and learning as acts of knowing. Freire’s approach to dialogue should be understood in the context of the massive 
inequality and oppression that shaped Brazil during the colonial and  post-colonial 
eras, and that persisted during his lifetime, and in relation to the struggle for political, 
cultural  and  psychological  liberation . This  context  is  sometimes  overlooked 
by  commentators  who  dismiss  Freire’s  approach  as  “too  political”. It  is  worth 
remembering that slavery in Brazil was abolished only in 1888 and that democracy 
was a fledgling idea and movement during Freire’s for mative working years in the 
1950s and ‘60s. For  Freire,  dialogue  is  not  merely  an  educational  technique;  it  is  something 
fundamental to the process of becoming a human being: “Dialog ue is the encounter 
between human beings, mediated by the world, in order to name the world” (Freire, 
1972: 61). Like Buber, he sees it as an act of communication in relationship that 
shapes one’s orientation to others and the world (Freire & Shor 1987: 98: “Dia logue 
is a moment where humans meet to reflect on their reality as they make and remake 
it”.) Unlike Buber, he emphasizes the relation between dialogue and political action; 
dialogue is not simply talking for its own sake. It is part of a praxis of transforming 
the world: “Through dialogue, reflecting together on what we know and don’t know, 

