In  order  for  informal  learning  to  be  successfully  imple-
mented,  an  organizational  culture  is  required  that   actively 
supports the learner in his learning activities. However, real-
ity is often far removed from this. The study by the Vodafone 
Foundation also comes to a sobering result here: “Not even a 
third of those surveyed (29%) feel, for example, that further  
education and learning are values lived by their employer. In 
personnel interviews, this topic only plays a role for a quarter 
of  the  employees  surveyed  (26%). Only  a  very  few  (8%)  see 
the learning culture in their company as good or very good” 
(Vodafone Foundation, 2016, p. 6). Perhaps even more than formal learning, informal learn-
ing depends on a real learning culture. It is obvious that the 
development  of  a  company-wide  learning  culture  cannot  be 
achieved  by  a  learning  companion  alone. A  culture  change 
always requires a broad alliance of all stakeholders, such as 
corporate  management,  executives,  HR,  works  council,  etc. The  task  of  the  learning  companion  can  and  should  be  to 
give impulses. 115

