Chapter 6 · Informal Learning

The starting point of the Learning Revolution are the effects 
of  digitalization:  Consistent  custo mer  orientation  and  ever 
shorter  innovation  cycles  require  agile  work  instead  of  clas-
sical  line  functions. Agile  work  in  turn  requires  agile  learn-
ing. The practice of informal learning is very similar to this: 
Informal  learning  “happens”. Since  the  learners  themselves 
decide  what  they  want  to  learn,  learning  usually  follows  the 
principle  “learning  is  what  helps”  rathe r  than  a  sophisti-
cated didactic plan. Typical is that phases of “self-learning” 
alternate with phases of “learning with and through others” 
(Social  Learning). Karlheinz  Pape  describes  such  learning 
as  “learning  in  networks”. In  his  blog  he  writes  about  this: 
“Every new challenge at work has to be mastered. Either you 
try it out, or you ask colleagues, or you look for documents, 
books,  or  other  sources  of  knowledge  until  you  have  solved 
your   current  problem  –  and  can  act  accordingly”  (Pape, 
2016). The  “7th  Annual  Learning  in  the  Workplace  Survey”  by 

education expert Jane Hart confirms this assessment:
»  The  5  most  valued  ways  of   learning  at,  for  or  through  work 
are Daily work experiences, Knowledge sharing with teams as 
well as Web resources, Web search and Professiona l Networks 
and  Communities. These  are,  in  fact,  all  self-organized 
(and  self-managed)  forms  of  learning. Whereas,  traditional 
organized  learning  initiatives  like  Classroom  training  and 
E-Learning  sit  right  at  the  bottom  of  the  list  along   with 
Conferences  and  other  events. Feedback  and  guidance  from 
managers  and  coaches,  as  well  as  internal  resources  fall 
squarely in the middle of the results (Hart, 2018a). Thus, learning does not follow a linear process, but consists 
of  various  activities  that  can  be  highly  interrelated. In  such 
a scenario, the distinction between “learning” and “working” 
