schools. In  addition  to  the  issue  of  resource  mismatch,  the  strictness  of 
the  reward  and  evaluation  system  in  the  group  varies  for  the  te achers 
from  different  schools. For  public  schools,  the  group  can  only  make 
few  adjustments  in  their  existing  administrative  management  system,  so 
the  group  implem ents  a  performance  incentive  system  instead  to  raise 
teachers’  moral. Teachers  from  public-entrusted  schools  (S2)  stated  that 
the  incentive  system  of  the  group  can  improve  teachers’  initiative  and 
teach ing  enthusiasm,  and  even  their  quality  of  life  to  some  extent. The  S  Education  Group  has  introduced  an  additional  teachers’  perfor-
mance  assessment  to  motivate  our  initiative  and  enthusiasm. This  assess-
ment  is  divided  into  ﬁrst,  second,  and  third  prizes  based  on  a  proportional 
hierarchy,  with  appropriate  cash  rewards. This  is  a  signiﬁcant  change  for 
our  school. (A6,  S2,  interview  expert) 

