"According to Florestan Fernandes, within what two-part strategic framework should the educational reforms that workers desire be pursued?","ing can be 
done, struck at despondency, and corrected the view that the only way to fight is by 
smothering the economic-corporative agenda within on eself. PRELIMINARY CONCLUSIONS

This article has argued that the dominant sectors have a keen interest in education for 
the mass of workers. However, since they are dependent and associated, the factions 
in  the  ruling  bloc  are  unable  to  carry  forward  a  project  for  the  nation   or,  more 
specifically, a project for public education in Brazil. As a result, the future of public 
education lies in the hands of the class that works for its living. The educational 

119

R. LEHER & P. VITTORIA

reforms that the workers want are unacceptable to the ruling bloc and must therefore 
be carried out i n the framework of the “revolution inside the order,” as part of the 
strategy of “revolution outside the order,” in the terminology of Florestan Fern andes 
(Fernandes, 1981). The veritable assault by business on the state’s education system—an offensive 
that has the support of the federal government in its Education Develo pment Plan—
is  intended  to  create  a  pro-establishment  horizon  for  Brazilian  education. From 
the methods for diagnosing educational problems based on centralized assessment 
systems  that  are  claimed  to  be  scientific  to  curriculum   reform,  teacher  training 
and school management strategies, all these measures are part of the agenda of a 
movement  led  by  corporations,  as   highlighted  above. Today  more  than  ever,  the 
situation cannot be reversed merely through strictly educational struggles carried 
out by education workers and studen ts. Such struggles can only be effective in the 
context  of  major  anti-establishment  movements,  and  so  there  is  a  contemporary 
resonance in the  ideas of Brazilian intellectuals like Florestan Fernandes, when he 
said that a new starting point was needed for educational struggles, and Paulo Fr eire, 
when he highlighted the political dimension of education and the need to organize 
for a critical-emancipatory type of education. The study of and dialog with the social movements that have been responsible for 
the most important struggles show that they have worked hard at prod ucing their own 
original knowledge, in order to criticize the bases of the capitalist lifestyle and to 
offer alternatives to capitalist society. A common trait in many of these movements is 
the belief that, in view of the striking asymmetry of strength and operational means 
between the workin g and the dominant sectors, the priority is to expand political 
training for the working class as a whole. Since 2014, the Marxism and Education Study Collective (Coletivo de Estudos 
em Marxismo e Educação—COLEMARX) in the Faculty of Education at Rio de 
