"According to a recent OECD study, how do the unemployment rates compare between Brazilians who completed secondary education and those who did not?","lain the processes of the natural world and society. A recent OECD study5 reports that the unemployment rate among Brazilians who 
did not complete secondary education (4.7% of the active population) is  lower than 
among those who did complete it (6.1%). It should be noted that two-thirds of the 
population aged 25–64 did not complete secondary education and yet, despite the low 
school achievement of  its adult population, the country remains one of the foremost 
commodity-driven emerging economies. That is typical of dependent capitalism, in 
which the so-called modern exists alongside the old, as Florestan Fernandes pointed 
out (Fernandes, 1975 ). This “dumbing-down” of education for children and young people cannot be seen 
in isolation from the concentration and centralization of the mass medi a, which operate 
in a similar way to the hegemonic pedagogy.6 In fact, the cultural education received 
by the vast majority of Brazilian children an d young people is a virtual monopoly in 
the hands of the dominant sectors. This process of capitalist control over the education 
system is so entrenched that the federal government (in the Education Development 
Plan)  and   the  state  and  municipal  governments  (in  their  audits  of  the  foundations 
run by the business movement, such as Airton Senna, Bradesco, Itaú- Cultural, Vitor 
Civita, Roberto Marinho, Santander, and Gerdau, among others) now naturally assume 
that those who are most entitled to pronounce on  education are the major corporations 
in the “All for Education” movement, which is made up of agribusiness and mining 
corporations, the media and te lephone/internet providers, publishers, and banks and 
financial institutions. If the dominant sectors are taking over public education, it is clearly because they 
realize that the intellectual and moral direction taken by the b loc in power is relevant 
to capital reproduction, especially in basic education, which is precisely where the 
state is still responsible for a large  proportion of student places. Corporate  interest  in  education  also  stems  from  the  fact  that  anticapitalist 
movements see education as part of the revolutionary strategy  for the twenty-first 
century. To prevent workers from making public education a space in which it is 
not the state that does the educating but educators who have organized themsel ves 

