"According to Gramsci's educational framework, what foundational skills and knowledge are expected to be imparted to students during the initial years of their schooling?","Within  the  capitalist  system,  therefore,  it  is 
essential that the state provide the funding necessary for a complete system of public 
educatio n that is free for all students, thereby moving what had been private into 
the public sector. That will be the only way to prevent the school system from being 
segmented by class, caste, ethnicity, and gender. Gramsci’s idea is for a full-time school with dormitories, refectories, specialized 
libraries, suitable rooms for research, and workshops for theoret ical-practical work. Funding must be sufficient to allow for the right ratio of students to teachers, since 
a school with fewer students per teacher will be more efficien t. That of course raises 
the problem of public funding, and the drastic cuts in the education ministry budget 
in particular. Gramsci’s  unitary  school  corresponds  to  elementary  and  middle  education 
(together known as basic education in Brazil). The early years should impart the 
basic, “instrumental” notions of schooling—reading, writing, arithmetic, geography, 
history—as well as a knowledge  of rights and duties, in other words “the first notions 

110

THE COMMODIFICATION OF EDUCATION IN BRAZIL

of  the  state  and  society.”  The  rema inder  of  the  students’  basic  education  should 
take place by other pedagogical means in creative schools based on self-discipline, 
research, an d theoretically unlimited intellectual and moral independence. Middle 
school should educate the young through research seminars, breaking the monopoly 
on  this  method  held  by  the  academic  world. Learning  should  be  carried  on  in 
seminars, libraries, and experimental laboratories, which should provide the basic 
essentials  for  vocational   orientation. A  close  relationship  thus  emerges  between 
education and work, but conceived in terms of the worker and not the market. As emphasized in Notebook 9 “Le Quistioni Scolastiche” (“Scholastic issues”—
Gramsci,  1977,  pp. 1183–1187),  the  unitary  school  corresponds  to  a  project  to 
educate the subordinate classes to be the leaders and the ruling class in society  as a 
whole, and not isolated individuals. The unitary school concept and proposal relate 
to the formation of class consciousness, which does not happen spontaneously, nor is 
it possible with out breaking away from the dominant ideologies. That does not mean 
that consciousness is something external to the workers that must be instilled in their 
minds by intellectuals. Stating that consciousness is not spontaneous is also a way of 
critiquing the economistic thesis that economic struggles per se make it possible to 
