We  invited  a  30-year  veteran  educator,  whose  career  ranges  from  intermediate 
school through higher education. This Caucasian administrator currently works as a 
building principal at a middle school in a middle class to upper middle class North 
Texas suburban  district. The administrator was initially taken aback by the atrocities 
in the case study, and reported that her first reaction was that this situation would 
 not occur in her school or district. However, she added that she quickly realized 
the faultiness of this belief and began to ponder what she would do if faced with a 
similar scenario. She said that above all she would be “crushed.”

The first action this principal would take is to contact the district-level media 
personnel  in  ord er  to  obtain  the  most  accurate  and  timely  information  available. The next step she would take is to activate the “flight team” that is on call for any 
crisis situation. This team of counselors and district professionals are deployed to 
a campus whenever necessary. A safe room is created for both students and adults; 
teachers and administrators are available to cover for the adults affected by tragedy. She has experienced the efficacy of the flight team first hand on multiple occasions 
upon student deaths. As preventative measures, the administrator expressed the expectation that the 
