What did the organizers of the group sharing activity hope their classmates would come to understand about how people develop the way they think and act?,"Those in the Elementary program sat on one side while those in the Educational 
Leadership program sat on the other. We really wanted our classmates to be exposed 
to new ideas and ways of thinking so grouping in this manner was the most ideal for 
our activity. After our classmates got situated into their groups, we asked them to briefly share 
a little about their individual upbringings. Specifically, to share stories about their 
family life, school life, religious life, etc. After our classmates shared in their small 
groups, we brought them together to share as a class. The questions we asked to 
promote discussion were:
•  What similarities did you notice in your group? •  What differences did you notice in your group? •  What are some of the different lenses you noticed emerge in your group? •  How do you think these experiences have shaped you/the people in your group? The goal of this activity was to help our classmates realize that all of the people 
in the room think and behave certain ways due to their own life e xperiences. We 
wanted to have our classmates begin to realize that we are primed to act in certain 
ways because of prior experiences. The lens that we view the world through is made 
up of these experiences. Being aware of our own lens is the first step to recognizing 
that the world does not operate as a binary system. This helps us to see that there 
is no “right way vs. wrong way” because we all come to the situation with different 
backgrounds. Word Choice

In our word association activity, we studied if certain words ignite an emotion in 
people that persuades opinion. News stories that are reported to us can be deciphered 
to have subliminal meanings due to the choice of words used to describe the situation. 128

