The shooting in Craven Heights, Texas offers an opportunity to further explore the 
immediate and long-term effects of such an incident on the lo cal, state, and national 
community. The victim was a 12-year-old boy – an age that could place the student 
in  either  a  primary  or  secondary  grade  level;  implications  for  how   both  school 
communities will react should be considered. When considering the Case Study, we explored the implications for responses that 
different grade levels would have with such an event. There also lies the important 
discussion about why this event even happened in the first place. We believe each 
discussion is important as we prepare to enter the schools and will be working with 
parents, students, community members, and fellow  colleagues in times of crisis. To 
start, we will first explore the elementary perspective. One of the main issues brought up by the case study is the police’s relationship 
to the community. With schools serving as a “hub” of the community, we need to 
address what preventative actions may have helped build trust between the police 
office rs and the citizens. We want to encourage police officers to have a positive 
presence in the elementary schools. It is necessary for police officers to build trust 
with students and staff in times of non-crisis. Whether that is through reading to 
students or participating in conversations in the morning, it is important for police 
officers to be available fo r any questions throughout the day. When considering how to address a crisis issue with students, we think a school-
wide crisis plan will be beneficial. This plan would involve monthly staff meetings 
to  discuss  potential  issues  and  continuously  reflect  on  effective  and  ineffective 
