"According to the group of future community leaders, what condition needed to be met beforehand for community members to feel motivated to show up and openly discuss a tragic event?","The future 
administrators  seemed  to  be  confused  by  this  question. In  such  an  emotional 
conversation,  should  these  dialogues  be  planned? Is  there  a  specific  end  goal  in 
mind before discussion? The group responded by mentioning that although there 
may not be a specific end goal, it is important for everyone to be included at an equal 
level. In other words, every person’s two cents holds the same weight in the overall 
discussion. The group of future community leaders also spoke about how important it would 
be to enter any kind of discussion on an even playing field. This situation could only 
be made possible if the members of the community felt they had a purpose prior to 
such a tragic situation. If community members felt that they didn’t have a say, they 
would not care, or see the point to show up to any sort of gathering to openly discuss 
t he tragedy. This group also mentioned that a big part of dealing with this situation 
has to do with preventative measures. Meaning that the community would have had 
to be somewhat connected prior to an act of police brutality in order to recover. However, it is also a possibility if these preventative measures were taken, this case 
study may have never happened. In many ways, such a tragedy shows that it might 
be too late for a community to be truly connected and promote empowerment. This 
has been played out recently through acts of police brutality that have somewhat 
mirrored the happenings portrayed in the case study. The future teachers discussed their community plan through the focal point of 
their students. They talked about how important it was for teachers to be there for 
their  students. This  could  be  anything  from  having  a  group  discussion  with  the 
class to reaching out to others in the school such as counselors to step in  for more 
serious issues. The future teachers also mentioned that this plan would look different 
depending on the specific group of students. 124

