tives in terms of their origins in the history of science, and as nomina lised accounts
of processes. Meaning-making in science involves reasoning that is sequential but
can be grounded in narrative, even if this reasoning is more formally codiﬁed thro ugh
abstracted visual and symbolic sign systems than is often the case in drama. Thus,
explaining the movement of electrons in an electric circuit (see Chap. “Drama-
