cultural adaptatIon

As their time in the Chinese language program in their schools continued, 
the  Chinese  teachers  experienced  a   cultural  shift  from  relying  on  and 
expecting automatic power as ‘the teacher’, to mimicking local teachers’ 
practices. They spoke about how they attempted to ‘walk down from the 
altar of being a teacher’, and accept that they needed to operate at the 
students’ level  and act in a manner that was ‘down to the earth’. They 
allowed students to see their vulnerability, stimulating their interest with 
humour rather than seeing humour as undermining their teacher-stud ent 
hierarchical relationship. On the surface, the data showed a degree of cul-
tural  adaptation  was  occurring  within  the  Chinese  teachers. However, 
Shen,  another  Chinese  teacher,  expressed  an  idea  that  invited  further 
investigation. She  expressed  that  she  liked  and  appreciated  the  way  her 
mentor teacher managed the class, particularly those who needed atten-
tion,  but   her  commitment  to  local  teaching  practices  and  culture  was 
superficial  for  her. She  said:  “It  is  like  our  celebration  of  Christmas. I 
enjoyed the decorations, atmosphere and the relaxed time, and I followed 
what the local people did, but I am not sure I share the same level of em o-
tional  attachment  to  it”. For  Shen,  internalising  the  culture  was  not  a 
simple process of being involved in the activity. This shift to develop alternative teaching and learning pedagogies and 
a localised teacher-student relationship in a new cultural context might be 
s een as a strategic one for survival. The Chinese teachers made concerted 
efforts  to  change  themselves  in  response  to  their  new  cultural  setting. Humans have the ability to learn to adjust to a new environment. They 
can be very quickly cognizant of new cultural practices in a new context, 
and over history have learnt to ‘do as local people do’. However, if such 
an ability or competence to adapt to the environment is regarded as cul-
tural dynamics, the claim might be too superficial given th at real cultural 
change is not at the level of observing what cultural agents do or if they 
