IntroductIon: ‘伦理’ (lunli) and ‘关系’ (guanxi)

As di scussed in Chap. 2, one aspect of Chinese theorising is the societal 
dimension of culture; it is paralleled with culture’s spiritual and material 
dimension  (Liang   2005). To  explore  the  research  participant  teachers’ 
practice  in  terms  of  positioning  themselves  and  their  students  in  their 
Australian clas srooms, it is necessary to review ‘伦理’ (lunli: ethics) and  
‘关系’ (guanxi: relationship) from linguistic and cultural aspects. These 
are  two  important  Chinese  concepts  when  looking  into  human-human 
relationships. Although closely related, they are in fact different. ‘伦理’ (lunli)

As discussed ‘伦理’ (lunli: ethics) is a concept describing a set of rules 
and  principles  when  addressing  human-human  guanxi  (Ding   2016). Chinese ‘伦理’ (lunli) initially was the guiding light for people to follow 
when managing relationships with family members; it was a method to 
