CHAPTER 2

I  see  the  de sire  for  sameness  even  within  the  community  and  neighboring 
communities where I teach. All schools within a nearby county are encouraged to 
teach the same novels suggested by the state to each class. They are encouraged 
to differentiate in their teaching, yet expected at the same time to participate in 
a one-size-fits-all education where the same  book is appropriate for an honors 
class and a special education inclusion class. One administrator said to a teacher 
that ideally, all schools in the state would agree to teach the same novels so that 
we could know that all stude nts were getting the same education. There are too 
many things wrong with this statement for me to even begin to break it down 
in  this  particular  chapter,  but  I  will  say  that  f ear  of  difference  and  fear  of  not 
being able to control what goes on in individual classrooms is partly behind this 
extreme  form  of  standar dization. Many  administrators  feel  more  comfortable 
knowing that the books that we teach our students all have the same approved 
message. Books like Julia Alvarez’s In the Time of the Butterflies are considered 
threatening and were pulled from circulation in some counties in Georgia bec ause 
of  the  portrayal  of  revolutionaries  who  are  celebrated  for  going  against  their 
oppressors  in  a  powerfully  corrupt  government. Apparently  there  is  fear  that 
through this difference we could accidentally nurture revolutionaries in our own 
