requirements so that she could get the best grade possible and be gr aded in the 
same  way  as  her  classmates. At  the  same  time,  I  told  the  student  to  keep  her 
original copy, the one of which she was so proud. I told her that this copy was the 
REAL version of her poem, and the one that she should keep forever and hang on 
her refrigerator. I told her that she may come to like the revised copy as well, but 
that the initial version with all of her passion and personality was priceless and  to 
never throw it away. Did I do the right thing? I still do not know. Some may say that I should have 
differentiated for this student by allowing her to bypass the rubric and turn in her 
original work. Others may say that allowing her to skirt around the rules like this 
would  teach  her  that  she  can  ultimately  ignore  directions  and  simply   do  as  she 
pleases. I do not believe that there is a clear-cut answer as to how I should have 
reacted in this instance. I do, however, believe that a clear-cut solution as to how this 
could have been prevented is that students need to have a space to be able to create  
passionately  and  personally. The  assignment  should  have  let  students  get  carried 
away with their own memories and their own stories and not have reined them back 
in with  a set of constrictive rules. By making her conform to the rigid assignment, I 
