"What kinds of contextual factors can shape a teacher's pedagogical strategies and instructional choices, and in what way do professional training experiences contribute to altering a teacher's approach to instruction?","In this study, we used teacher cognitions as 
the theoretical underpinning for developing narrative prompts and interview 
questions. Informed by this conceptualization, we understand teacher cognitions as a 
complex and dynamic network of knowledge and experiences. First, teacher 
cognitions are negotiating with and responsive to contextual factors, including 
those sociocultural, institutional, curricular, situa tional, and personal (Burri et 
al., 2017). Contextual factors affect teachers’ cognitions, regulating their peda-
gogical strategies and decisions. For example, Feryok (2008) witnessed that an 
English language teacher adjusted her plan of maximizing students’ speaking 
time because she was cogniz ant of the institute’s test-passing requirements 
and limited instructional time and available resources. In another study, sur-
veying 43 Chinese as Second Language teachers, Gong et al. (2018) reported 
that  the  participants’  varied  perceptions  on  the  ways  to  cultivate  students’ 
intercultural  communicative  competence  cor responded  with  the  socioeco-
nomic context of the university, computer availability on campus, academic 
resources, and interpersonal relationships  with their colleagues or superiors. Second, teacher cognitions are largely shaped by their professional training 
experiences. For instance, Shi et al. (2019) found that a group of Chinese EFL 
teachers’ understanding of how to teach writing changed from a product-ori-
ented and grammar-correction-hea vy approach to a communicative approach 
after attending a training workshop where they participated in class lectures, 
discussions, and lesson plan  designs. The study also noted that although the 
teachers showed growth in understanding the new pedagogy, some of them 
were not able to incorporate this appr oach due to curricular restrictions fully. - 978-90-04-54964-7
