Durkheim pointed out that it is through education that individuals internalize 
the social norms, rules, and discipline whi ch enable collective solidarity; it is 
education that selects and allocates talents; and it is education that teaches the 
knowledge and skills neces sary for occupational and social roles (Durkheim, 
1956). Functionalist  sociology  emphasized  socialization  and  social  allocation 
as key functions of education and paid more attention to higher educatio n. Parsons and Platt (1970) discussed how the growth of participation in higher 
