Dobbins  and  Knill’s  (2014)  res earch  findings  are  broadly  supported  by  a 
substantial body of analysis from other researchers. Middlehurst and Teixeira 
(2012) provide a complementary overview of recent trends and challenges in 
governance within the EHEA. They argue that the driving force behind conver-
gent trends is the shared recognition that higher education systems are central 
to the achievement o f Europe’s economic and social goals. Higher education 
institutions (HEIs) are expected to advance knowledge, provide a skilled labor 
force, promoted social equality, contribute to perso nal development and cul-
tural enrichment, and provide a range of other external benefits such as better 
personal health and a stronger environmental  commitment. The prioritization 
of instrumental over personal and cultural objectives together with the mas-
sive expansion of the systems and the associated risi ng costs have inspired a 
“modernization agenda” which has focused especially on the need to change 
governance mechanisms, challenging the traditiona l sovereignty of intellec-
tual and professional expertise, and legitimating the greater influence of exter-
nal stakeholders. They suggest that a significant feature of the modernization 
agenda can be characterized as “less government but more governance” (Mid-
dlehurst & Te ixeira, 2012, p. 538) whereby the state designs a framework of rules 
and policy objectives for the overall system. Greater institutional autonomy is 
counterbalanced by an increase in competition between institutions and by 
enhanced quality assurance mechanisms wh ereby HEIs are held accountable 
to society for their results. Typically, the new governance arrangements involve 
an increasing number of stakeholders and specialist agencies. Several analysts have concurred in the judgment that while there is clear 
