and  therefore  inte rest  satisfaction  and  of  course  dissatisfaction,  if  the  student 
concerned was aware enough to be dissatisfied and in position to articulate i t. Then 
to there is perceptual subconscious susceptibility to racialized-classist stereotypes, 
and other pseudoscience theories such as the “bell curve .”

Learning how not to learn (resistance/push back) is both an intellectual and social 
test  of  will  that  requires  on  the  part  of  the  stude nt  concerned,  very  purposefully 
active work by design, that is to say a state of social being, habit, and willful state 
of obscenest. However, learning not to learn occurs in the context of social reality 
for that student. It brings with it implications related to social economic circumstance, 
the unconscious – conscious awareness, interpretation, and experiences within  that 
social reality. There is that uncertainty among some students as to the relevance of education 
relative to their racialized-gendered self; via, for instance, masculi nity, sexuality, 
sense of manliness that they are striving to represent in the process and possibilities 
of becoming. I would suggest to you all that, emotionally laden fits and resistance 
attitudes and behaviors can represent cultural mistrust, not just obscenest at titude 
