a general class of objects. Therefore, a student can use this exemplar or generic
example to help them understand the general characteristics of an entire class of
objects. Within the coding instrument, statement types and justiﬁcation types are inde-
pendent dimensions. This separation is due to the fact that general and particular
statements can both be justiﬁed by empirical or deductive arguments. To highlight
this difference, Otten and colleagues used the terms “general” and “particular” to
refer only to statements and the terms “deductive” and  “empirical” to refer only to
justiﬁcations. In the same manner, I use these terms as codes for statement and
justiﬁcation types respectively. 4.3.4 Classifying Justiﬁcation Types in Textbook

