personal  accountability  and  ethics  of  caring. According  to  Collins,  “such  power  is 
transformative in that Black women’s relationships with children and other vulnerable 
community members ar e not intended to dominate or control. Rather, their purpose is to 
bring people along...so that vulnerable members of the community will be able to attain 
the self-reliance and independen ce essential for resistance” (Collins, 2000, p. 209). Additionally, another facet of community mothering is the “mothering of mind,” 
which describes the familiar relationship between African American wom en teachers 
and  their  Black  students. The  relationship  developed  goes  beyond  traditional 
mentoring  and  blurs  the  lines  of  traditional  teacher-student  relationship. It  often 
involves intimate dialogue, cultural sharing and caring, and personal responsibilities. “Like the mother-daughter relationship, this “mothering of the mind” among Black 
women seeks to move toward the mutuality of a shared sisterhood that  binds African-
American women as community othermothers” (Collins, 2000, p. 207). Beauboeuf-
Lafantant (2002) discusses three characteristics of Black women teachers who engage 
in mothering of mind pedagogy: 1) Embrace of the mater nal (committed to social 
justice at all cost); 2) political clarity (recognizing systemic societal and educational 
injustices), and 3) ethic of risk  (advocating against oppressive in a manner that serves 
the community). She concludes with “womanist teachers see themselves as dynamic 
agents of social justice precisely because they define themselves as having a sense 
 of connection with and responsibility to the human struggle for freedom and justice” 

