"How did the author's experiences in Indonesia lead them to interpret the phrase ""rumah kedua"" in two distinct ways?","CHAPTER 6

RUMAH KEDUA

The title for this vignette: Rumah kedua refers to a special part of the world that has 
informed my thinking deeply in the pa st decade or so. I am most fortunate to have 
had very rich opportunities to live and work in the different regions of Indonesia: 
first, in developing and implementin g an international field school in Sulawesi and 
later; as an international consultant on development projects in the education sector 
(based in Jaka rta). Both experiences caused me to consider this term in a dual way: 
first, as I spend a lot of enjoyable time in this part of the world I have come to re gard 
Indonesia as my own rumah kedua (second home) as I am always warmly welcomed 
by the community while working there. Second, the lessons I have learned from this 
region inform a ‘second way’ of viewing our home (or world) economy. My own story in Indonesia began at a meeting with my university’s Vice-President 
International  some  years  ago  as  he  summarized  my  University’ s  development 
efforts  over  a  lunchtime  meeting  –  just  prior  to  my  design  of  an  international 
field school that would leverage our subs tantial connections in the region. The VP 
first  described  to  me  the  role  of  my  university  in  leading  the  Eastern  Indonesia 
Universities Development Project (EIUDP) from 1 987–2002: a project funded by 
the  Canadian  International  Development Agency. The  project  aimed  to  promote 
long-term linkages between Indonesian and Canadian institutions. This was done by 
creating Faculties of Science and establishing Language Centres at five universities 
in the region. One of these, Universitas Sam Ratulangi (in Manado) was to become 
the base for a successful field school I designed after travelling in the region. Since 
that initial trip, the field school has run three times, the experiences of which has 
transformed the lives of many students (and their instru ctor). The first night we were there, a bunch of us caught our first Indonesian sunset. I remember us being in awe at the colours of the sky, the Manado Tua volcano 
backdrop and the calmness of the Celebes Sea … (Field school student)

