(1890) who traveled extensively throughout the region in the mi d eighteenth century 
and those of traders who colonized much of modern day Indonesia in their search for 
(indigenous) spices such as cloves and nutm eg. Indonesia offers rich opportunities 
to  explore  intercultural  approaches  in  education. The  courses  (delivered  in  close 
cooperation with local partners) involve short or extended visits to local communities, 
national parks, meetings  with community organizations, exchanges with university 
students, cultural events, presentations by university representatives, and volunteer 
work   with  community  organizations. As  such  I  have  had  ample  opportunity  to 
interact with Indonesians within their local communities and this has impacted my 
thinking profoundly  about the concept of ‘home’. LEARNING ‘IN PLACE’

Locating a field school in the Minahasa region of Sulawesi was an ideal design for 
modeling place-based education as the region  must be experienced to be understood: 
First, the region has long been recognized as a ‘biodiversity hotspot’ by scientists 
and is located within the  famed ‘coral triangle’ – regarded as the epicenter of marine 
biodiversity on the planet. Second, located on the northern tip of an island, Sulawesi 
is surrounded by other small volcanic island groups to the north and south and is 
bathed  by the warm currents of the Celebes sea. Learning  activities  designed  for  the  field  school  allow  students  to  build 
community  while  also  experiencing  diverse  natural  settings   such  as  the  active 

