volcanoes  of  Lokon  and  Mahawu  in  the  highlands,  to Tangkoko  Nature  Reserve 
(a protected lowland  forest) and Pulau Bunaken (with its fringing coral reefs). To 
me  this  landscape  was  initially  wild  and  exotic,  but  after  repeated  visits  during 
later field schools, the scene has become familiar  and comforting to me. This new 
familiarity with my ‘rumah kedua’ has assisted me in critically reflecting on my 
own cultural interpretations of home. Culture and Environment

My ‘natural history’ adventures in the Indonesian field school typically last a few 
days or weeks as we explore the surround ing environment from a geological and/
or  biological  perspective. These  experiences  are  balanced  with  visits  to  sites 
such  as  Pasar  tradisional  (traditional  market)  in Tomohon  or Airmadidi  (with 
its   ancient  Minahasan  burial  crypts  or  Waruga). Many  activities  explore 
traditional local (sustainable) technologies such as: bamboo cooking, fish traps 
or other forms of traditional knowledge t hat have arguably been part of the local 
culture for thousands of years. Several indigenous groups are present in Minahasa 
and  in  the  highlands,  several  tribes  have  merged  to  form  the  dominant  group. Much of this intermingling was accelerated by the colonial presence of Dutch 
traders. For me, learning in a place like Indonesia draws on the functions, intersections, 
and  relations  of  a  critical  form  of  place-based  education,   as  we  analyze  the 
ongoing Dutch influence in the region as a historic spice-producing region for the 
Dutch  East  India  Company  (or  VOC)  thr ough  course  assignments. We  explore 
differences  between  colonial  relations  to  place,  and  other  (Indigenous)  relations 
to land, where a traditional ecological knowl edge of the land is integral to ‘being 
and knowing in the world’ (Meyer, 2008). These ideas are closely related to deeper 
indigenous perspective on the concept of ‘home’ discussed in earlier chapters. Despite  this,  a  colonial  relation  to  place  in  Minahasa  is  also  evident  here 
(alongside  Indigenous  perspectives)  as  the  large  clove   and  coffee  plantations 
persist in and around villages such as Rurukan. Despite the end to colonization in 
the  1950’s,  this  relationship remains  a  complex  one  as  many  Minahasans  fought 
with the Dutch (against t he Javanese) during the Indonesian war of Independence. Some elders for example, continue to believe that they enjoyed greater autonomy 
