in  the  region. It  increasingly  resembles  any  large  city  with  popular  shopping 
complexes,  fast  food,  chic  hotels  and  trendy  restaurants. As  the  city  grows,  so 
do  its  problems:  urban  encroachment  on  protected  areas,  solid  waste  (eg. plastic 
migrating to coral reefs), air pollution, and traffic congestion. In short, it faces many 
of the sustainability issues that larger urban areas face: how to balance development 
‘at  home’  without  compromising  the   important  ecological  and  cultural  capital 
present in the surrounding region. This  “urban”  adventure  is  an  important  program  activity  in  any  field  school: 
The act of simply living in and ‘experiencing’ the “urban pla ce” that is Manado. While living there, we do this by shopping or eating, interacting with local school 
children  as  we  teach  English  in  the  schools,  meeting  wit h  local  NGO’s  or  by 
attending lectures at the local campus of Universitas Sam Ratulangi. These activities 
provide  students  with  information  on  topics  such  as  ecology  and  natural  history, 
but  they  also  include  topics  of  a   more  urban  focus  including  issues  of  language 
and  culture,  public  health,  education  and  a  variety  of  other  development  issues. Figure 18. A simple rural home on the Island Manado Tua (old Manado)

