These are all related to the Indonesian concept of ‘home’. The intent here is to allow 
students to make connections between their everyday lives in the city and the issues 
in the surrounding physical/cultura l environments that support them. Overall, I believe that the inclusion of a form of ‘urban residency’ in the field 
schools  gives  students  a  greater  appreciation  of  the  divers ity  of  cultural  and 
environmental systems around them as well as an urge to “act” to overcome existing 
problems. Indeed,  it  is  a  synergy  of  universities,  schools,  and  community-based 
organizations  that  together  struggle  to  promote  more  sustainabl e  living  both  in 
Sulawesi and abroad. Still, common urban practices such as hyper-consumerism, 
environmental  depletion  and  socio-economic  inequality  need  to  be  seen  as 
contribut ing to environmental problems. Educators (as individuals or professionals) 
are expected to respond to the collective demand for a “greener” socially-just world 
