During  my  own  deep  reflection  while  working  in  Indonesia,  I  have  also  come 
to  understand  t hat  place-based  and  experiential  programs  help  to  examine  the 
true  complexity  of  our  homes  (and  the  systems  that  support  them). Ecological 
frameworks (such as the one described in this book) apply the principles of ecology – 
to  an  examination  of  the  relationship  of  all   living  things  with  their  environment 
and with one another as living and interdependent systems (Bowers, 1999). So, in 
a philosophical sense, ecological notions such as Kawanua (or deep community) 
apply to the conception of our human-world relationship. Ecological frameworks 
aim to build on a specific understanding: that humankind is an interconnected part 
of  both  the  human  and  natural  worlds. Further,  to  understand  ecologically  is  to 
make sense of the human world as part of, not apart from, nature; it is to understand 
humankind’s “im plicatedness in life” (Grunewald, 2003). For me, Indonesia also 
taught me that understanding ecologically has an emotional core: One’s knowledge 
about  ‘ecology’  is  meaningful  due  to  a n  emotional  attachment  to  the  world  and 
all of its living communities:

It was in Sulawesi that I found a true appreciation and realization as  to just 
how  interconnected  our  environment  is  …  Living  here  (in  Canada)  I  feel 
disconnected from my environment. In Indonesia, I knew where the banana 
I was eating came from as I saw the (Minahasan) boy cut it down from a tree, 
and I knew where my fish was from  as I saw it swimming in the ocean … (Field 
school participant)

As  a  final  reflection,  I  can  personally  attribute  the  implementation  of  t his  field 
school as a transformative moment in my own professional career:

