has  become  a  theoretical  basis  for  the  interdisciplinary  teaching  in  the 
virtual  environment. This  course,  based  on  the  ideas  of  the  Russian 
philosopher  G.P. Shchedrovitsky,  demonstrates  “the  teamwork  skill”  as  a 
pure  professional  competence  (Shchedrovitsky  1995;  Shchedrovitsky  and 
Popov  1994 ;  Sheinbaum  2007). However,  besides  being  able  to  “play  in 
a  team,”  the  competent  engineer  has  to  understand  who  manages  the 
team,  set  tasks  for  it ,  provide  it  with  the  necessary  means  and  resources 
and  determine  the  functionality  of  each  team  member. Nowadays  it 
can  be  achieved  by  studying  in  the  virtual  environment  as  a  part  of 
the  interdisciplinary  team. The  students  develop  systematical  thinking 
and  can  participate  not  only  in  the  design  of  industrial  equipment  and 
technologies,  but   also  in  the  design  of  the  professional  activity  itself. The  interdisciplinary  training  implemented  at  Gubkin  University  is  an 
effective  method  of  developing  the  students’  competence  in  team work. Beside  the  specialist’s  knowledge  about  his  own  functions  and  responsi-
