achieve a goal whic h would be beyond his unassisted efforts” (Wood, Bruner, & Ross, 1976, p. 90, cited 
by Barron et al., 1998, p. 276). Barron et al. (1998) used three types of scaffolds that were defined by 
Collins,  Brown,  and  Newman  (1989);  those  that  function  to  communicate  process,  t hose  that  provide 
coaching, and those that elicit articulation. Scaffolding tools such as video formats and problem materials 

