What strategies are proposed to address the workload burden caused by the production time limitation of desktop 3D printers used in anatomy education?,"other, cardiac 
(Lim, et al., 2016; Wang et al., 2017), liver (Kong et al., 2016) and skull (Chen 
et al., 2017) anatomy as well as applied anatomy su ch as liver disease (Percia & 
Sun, 2018). 3D prints proved to be equal or even superior to other resources 
including real human tissue. More generally, the application of 3D printing is part of a general trend 
towards inclusion of new technology to facilitate and improve learning and  
teaching as well as learners’ satisfaction in anatomy courses (Losco et al., 2017; 
Wilson et al., 2018). This technology enables the presentation of anatomical 
structures  in  a  physical  three  dimensions  –  the  preferred  format  in  teach-
ing a ha nds-on subject such as anatomy (Yammine & Violato, 2016). Several 
innovative ways of applying 3D printed models have been suggested, includ-
ing combining 3D printing with other “traditional” techniques of p roducing 
anatomical models (O’Reilly et al., 2016) and producing prints for individual 
students (Backhouse et al., 2019). 3D printing on desktop printers has certain constraints, the most prominent 
being the production time. However, this burden to workload can be reduced 
through increased collaboration between institutions (Štrkalj & Dayal, 2014) 
and by engaging student s in the process of printing (Ford & Minshall, 2019). Indeed, the actual process of 3D printing anatomical replicas, if innovatively 
