"What were the main functional goals guiding the design of school desks in the early twentieth century, and how much emphasis did designers place on their visual appeal?","It is this loud silence that we address in 
this chapter. Although  a  number  of  studies  have  focused  on  classroom  design  and  school 
furniture over time, the desk itself has rarely been the focus of  study until recent 
years. While O’Hare (1998) and Oyewole, Haight, and Freivalds (2010) discuss 
how certain seating plans and desk heights help promote discussion-based teachi ng 
and improve the comfort level for students in classrooms, the school desk is not 
a central feature for them. Currently, there is a growing literature addressing the 
materiality of educational space, “that is, the ways that objects are given meaning, 
how the y are used, and how they are linked into heterogeneous active networks, in 
which people, objects, and routines are closely connected” (Lawn & Grosven or, 
2005,  p. 7). Focusing  on  the  biographies  of  educational  materialities,  Herman, 
Van Gorp, Simon, and Depaepe (2011) explore how school desks were designed,  
produced, and ultimately used in classrooms in the first half of the twentieth century. They suggest that school desks were often designed to meet the requirements of 
school hygiene and safety, to be manipulated easily in daily practice,  and to help 
maintain  an  orderly  and  disciplined  school  life. Designers  paid  less  attention  to 
the aesthetic qualities of the desks. Even though new generations of the designers 
attempted  to  make  certain  material,  technical,  and  mechanical  improvements  to 
previous designs , the school desk evolved very slowly in practice. Constrained by 
“financial, organizational and other peripheral conditions,” the desk models that 
had the best chance to survive were “not the most a ttractive…but those that most 

