What distinction does the author draw between how her settler peers discuss their academic journeys and how she is expected to explain her own background when meeting new colleagues?,"Turns out, a lack of clean drinking water can truly 
bond  two  people! Nevertheless,  a  dear Anishinaabeg  friend  and  leader  once  told 
me, “To be Indigenous is to be inherently political.” I didn’t appreciate the we ight 
of this statement when she first shared this with me, but I’ve come to realize that 
even in life’s greatest moments you never escape this reali ty. As Indigenous women, 
the  legacy  and  normalization  of  settler  colonialism  permeates  even  our  fondest 
memories, as it comes knocking on our  door even when we don’t want to answer. STRAND II

