Aedon

I went to school in Port Coquitlam, a suburb of Vancouver, British Columbia, in the 
1980s. In Grade 9, I attended an unconventional French immersion programme. We took our core classes the first semester and, in the second semester, we were 
cloistered together, even taking recess and lunch at different times  from the rest of 
the school so as not to contaminate our pristine second-language environment. Not 
only was our schedule different from the rest of the student body, but so was our 
classroom set-up. We formed groups upon starting our work together. Each group sat at two large 
tables  topped  with  faux  wood  veneer  and  metal  legs,  which  together  formed  a 
hexagon (Figure 3). At the front right corner of the class, near the door and on the 
opposite side of the room from the windows, was my group. Every other classroom 
had one-piece desks with moulded plastic chairs. I remember other kids frequently 
popping their heads into the class in wonderment at our arrangement. Our  group  tables  were  sacrosanct. Not  only  were  they  personal  space  (I,  for 
example, would never sit in another group member’s place), but they were group 
space—somehow doubly  important. It was “notre place”. Personal belongings could 

