What two components of a teacher education program work simultaneously to help pre-service teachers explore and articulate the values that shape their professional identities?,"EXPLORING THE CURRICULAR  

POSSIBILITIES OF PRE-SERVICE TEACHER 

PROFESSIONAL IDENTITY

INTRODUCTION

There  is  a  growing  interest  in  professio nal  identity  development  for  pre-service 
teachers within teacher education programs. Professional identity informs pre-service 
teachers’ judgments and understandings of their roles and responsibilities and shapes 
the manner in which  they teach and interact with students (Walkington, 2005). The 
development of identity is a complex process supported by both theoretical course 
work and field practicums. These two contexts work simultaneously to encourage 
pre-service teachers to explore and articulate values that shape the development of 
their  profe ssional  identities  (Walshaw  &  Savell,  2001). In  this  chapter,  I  explore 
the  emergence  of  professional  identity  in  pre-service  teachers  as  well  as  the 
epistemological beliefs that  illuminate this aspect of self. Through semi-structured 
interviews across a two-year after degree teacher education program, I also investigate 
the manner in which pre-service teac hers move from students to professionals. The 
central question raised in this chapter involves how pre-service teachers understand 
their professional identities within particular educational  contexts. Pedagogical and discipline-specific knowledge is viewed as central to how pre-
service teachers approach their teaching roles and invest in learning e nvironments; 
however,  recent  literature  on  teacher  education  stresses  the  importance  of  the 
development of professional identity (Beaucham g & Thomas, 2009). Connected to 
pre-service teachers core values, professional identity shapes their teaching practices 
(Walkington, 2005). Thus, teacher educators can benefit from understanding how 
identity  develops  and  impacts  the  self-efficacy  and  commitment  of  pre-service 
te achers to teaching. The  initial  portion  of  this  chapter  reviews  the  theoretical  framework  for  the 
development  of  identity. Then  excerpts  from  interviews  with  six  pre-service 
teachers are shared to draw specific attention to the ways in which they reflect on 
their i nteractions and engagement with others and their perceptions of themselves as 
emerging teachers. The term ‘partner teacher’ is used here to refer to the professional 
teacher with whom the pre-service teacher is placed within the field practicum. It is 
synonymous with the terms ‘cooperating teacher’, ‘supervising teacher’ or ‘mentor 
teacher.’

