"According to Qian's conceptual framework, what is the essential difference between a ""C-ultured person"" and a ""c-ultured person""?","The inclusion of spirituality (not aligned to the Western notions 
of religion) also broadens the definition of Qian’s (2011) ‘C-ulture’. In 
this context Qian (2011) is referring to the morals, ethics, values and sen-
sibilities that develop at the level of one’s spirit; an invisible, i nternalisation 
of how to live as a ‘cultured person’ whilst mastering the knowledge of the 
Six Arts (see Chap. 5), and demonstrating this learning in their everyday 
lives—a ‘C-ultured person’. If a person masters the techniques and knowl-
edge  without  the  spirituality  and  condition  to  transform  the  world,  it 
could be argued that t hey are operating as a ‘c-ultured person’, for exam-
ple, a scholar who contemplates the world without interaction. ternary of cultural elements

Chinese philosopher and culture scholar Liang Shuming (2005) has theo-
rised culture as a ternary, comprising three dime nsions: spiritual, societal 
and material. Further Liang (2005) contends the generalisability of this 
theorisation holds true across cultures in the East and West. Liang’s (2005) ‘spiritual’ dimension refers to the development of the 
self, based on the knowledge gained through literacy, literature, philoso-
phy,  arts and aesthetics. This cultural layer develops the invisible relation-

