17

ship  with  one’s  self  as  a   human  transformed  in  and  of  higher  order 
thinking. This  resounds  with  Li’s  (2016)  ‘化人’  (huaren-transform 
human) and Qian’s (2011) ‘c-ulture’ as discussed above. Interactive relationships are the basis of what Liang (2005) refers to as 
the societal dimension of culture. This layer requires negotiating the rela-
tionships with others; the relationships with family, friends, communities, 
the nation and the world. It requires people to implement the spiritual in 
everyday life by enacting the cultural knowledge, ethical, political and eco-
nomic relationship wit h others. To a certain degree there is a blurring of 
the line between the spiritual and the societal, as relationships with others 
are based on the individual  person’s spiritual characteristics as these guide 
one’s behaviour with others. For Liang (2005) a person’s relationship with the material world is the 
third and last dimension of culture. One’s interaction with the physical 
world  centres  on  what  value  a  person  perceives  and  desires  in  terms  of 
food, clothes, housing and te chnology (mobile phones, computers). Throughout the discussion above, there are links across the conceptu-
alisations of ‘人化’ (renhua) (Li 2016), ‘c-ulture’ (Qian 2011) and ‘spiri-
