answer from outside the self but rather from searching one’s inner being 
through self-reflection. Aspiring to sensibility/morality can be empowered 
through reading literature, especially the classics, and appreciating art works. The second ‘理性’ (lixing) rationality is an ability to direct oneself to 
successfully live with the natural world, and to make sense of the physical,  
built world. The second ‘理性’ (lixing) can be gained through physics and 
maths books where the mind of a person can be developed through the 
use  of  equipment  a nd  negotiating  external  knowledge. Liang  (2005, 
p. 113) used ‘wrong’ and ‘false’ to further distinguish the two ‘理性’ (lix-
ing): one loses connection to the first ‘理性’ (lixing) (sensibility/morality) 
 if s/he steals others’ belongings (i.e. this behaviour is wrong); one loses 
the second ‘理性’ (lixing) (rationality) if s/he cannot deduce A and C’s 
relationship based on the condition of A= B, and B=C (i.e. the deduction 
is false). Thus the first ‘理性’ (lixing) is based on subjective knowing and 
the second ‘理性’ (lixing) is objective knowing. In the core of the culture, 
‘wrong’ is more serious than ‘false’ (Liang 2005, p. 115). Departing from 
either type of ‘理性’ (lixing) can occur in everyday life, however, the con-
sequences caused by a lack of rationality is minor, whereas  that caused by 
a lack of morality/sensibility is major. ‘伦理’ (lunli) ethIcs

