and the knowledge and use of the microscopes of the scientists and technic ians who 
worked in Gibbins department. Learning about these skills and practices influenced 
Truman’s later work. Artists and scientists learned from and with each other in these 
projects. Finding common ground and shared experience at a personal level helped 
enable artists and scientists learning through working together. References

