How do the representative and the wallflower differ in terms of their level of involvement with the class community and peer group?,"Thus, he or she risks becoming excluded by the other pupils and be not 
considered a friend and a mate by them. This is the aspect of her or his willingness 
to suffer which seems to be an essential element of figures of probation. The representative constitutes the midway in relation to the first two figures 
of probation. The difference to the class clown is that she or he adapts to the rules 
and possibilities of the class community and operates within them. Compared to 
the overachiever, she or he is enabled through his social position to do something 
for the class community. In contrast to the class clown and the overachiever, the 
references are not primarily outside the class community. To the representative, 
the class community and the peer group do not differ from each other, as the class 
is the target of her or his voluntarily en gagement. Thus, the representative proves 
her- or himself through this engagement with the whole community of the class. As the word “wall” already implies, the wallflower locates her- or himself at 
the edge of any bustling activity in a twofold respect. She or he is neither strongly 
engaged in the class community nor in the community of a peer group. The word 
“flower” points to the positionally low character of the wallflower’s attitude. In 
contrast to animals, plants show only a limited and uncoordinated reply to the 
environment. The wallflower proves her- or himself by not getting involved and 
thus remaining pure. In this respect she or he shows a loyalty to an non-existing 
reference-group which seems to be imagined or dreamt and located in an ideal world. The wallflower would estrange her- or himself from this ideal reference-group if 
she or he would get involved into the lower spheres of everyday life  in the present 
reality. Since the representative constitutes the midway between the class clown, 
being dominantly based on the peer group, and the overachiever, being based  on 
the class community only for a certain purpose, due to the representative’s spe-
cific structure of reference, the wallflower can be regarded as t he opposite of the 
representative. Considering the communities generated by the school structure, 
the representative is engaged in both kinds and the wallflower in none. 6.4 

