as a whole person. This scientific practice is restricted to the phase of reconstruc-
tion. It respects the autonomy of every-day life decisions. The second stage of professionalization is the acquisition by professions. An 
example of this is the therapeutic practice which is not only an increased, me-
thodical practice of reconstruction, but which is furthermore char acterized by “an 
intensification of pressure on decisions” (ebd. ), as it vicariously makes decisions 
for sick people who chose to be treated. Professions assume a scientifically based, methodically increased and vicar-
ious method of decision-making which is amenable to an intensified obliga tion 
for justification. This vicarious decision-making takes place in central areas of 
coexistence: the administration of justice and the psychosomatic integrity of the 
sin gle life. Both areas are in tension. With the distinction between the transfer of knowledge and norms and the 
therapeutic dimension of educational practice, Oevermann differentiates betw een 
