6.1 

The Human Being as a Probation-Seeker2

In short, the notion of the  human being as a probation-seeker means that from 
the age of one the individual tries to cooperate and do useful things by which 
she or he is convi nced that they are valuable and appreciable for the community. This perspective contradicts common opinions which interpret the human being 
as a need-satisfier or pleasure-seeker. The theoretical model of the first concept 
derives from the idea of the human being as a homo oeconomicus. The second one 
emanates from the impulse-theoretical concept of Sigmund Freud (Zizek 2015a). The concept of the probation-seeker is inspired by Ulrich Oevermann’s concept 
of the dynamic of probation which cannot be “shelved” (cf. Oevermann 1995, 2003). In the following I will explain the concept of the probation-seeker and expound 
three differences to Oevermann’s theoretical position. In the introduction I would like to clarify the question of what the concept of 
identity as a problem of the individual means. In the anorganic field we find relative 
identity in the sense of the identity of two things. In the organic field, the sphere 
of life, the concept of identity points to the problem of identifying a certain being 
and in the human field it ref ers to the problem of recognizing oneself. What are the pre-conditions of identifying an organism? When we try to 
identify an organism we do it by recognizing his unique profile. The concept of 

