"How does the author distinguish the concept of ""figure of probation"" from Oevermann's ""myth of probation,"" particularly with regard to whether the individual is consciously aware of the connection between their biographical experience and their behavioral patterns?","Thus, it recognizes itself 
by a social profile. The tension which results out of this cannot be put to aside or 
“shelved”. On the one hand there is uniqueness as a pre-condition of identification, 
and on the other hand the subject depends on the recognition of the value o f its 
contribution by the community. I coined the concept of the figure of probation during a sequence-analysis of 
an interview with a male student who was in his first term at that time . In this 
interview the student emphasized the simultaneity of his family’s move to a small 
council flat, followed by the insolvency of their shop and  his enrollment, through 
which he sought, despite these family burdens, to successfully acquire a new social 
context. This experience cumulated in a figure of probation, which profiled him 
in his primary group in a specific manner: I am the one who despite the failur e 
of my primary group manages to acquire new social contexts autonomously and 
successfully. I interpreted this biographical experience of successfully acquiring 
new social contexts and thereby escaping a restrictive background, as a dynamica l 

Handling Probat﻿ion-Seekers 

91

element of his figure of probation. In addition to his very successful study, this 
dynamic element is expressed in the interesting fact that he usually likes to leave his 
student apart ment very early in the morning to prepare for seminars in the open air. I do not assume that the student was aware of the relation between this biograph-
ical experience and his habit of going outside early in the morning. This stresses 
a central difference between the concept figure of probation and Oevermann’s 
reflexive concept myth of probation. The reflexive character of myths of probation 
becomes clear in the following quotation:

“Who am I, where do I come from, where do I go to? It is this Trinity of questions, 
whose necessary reply in the end as well, referring both to the collectivity and to the 
individual, constitutes the  universal function of a myth” (Oevermann 1995, p. 35).3

Following Oevermann the consciousness of finiteness, which is induced by lan-
guage, generates the “… universal problem of the dynamic of proba tion which 
can´t be shelved.” (Oevermann 2003, p. 341). Language allows us to address what 
is not present and thus to step out of the here and now in which the animal lives. By the example of one’s own parents and one’s own children, we experience that 
