"According to Gladston's arguments, how can the translation process alter or distort the perceived meanings of a source language's cultural concepts, and what consequences does this have for cross-cultural understanding?","The conceptual, cultural and underlying philosophical 
meanings  are  diluted  and  often  a  new  incorrect  meaning  is  generated 
through the tran slation (Gladston 2016). The onus is then on the translator. Loss of original meaning may not be 
a subjective choice on the part of the translator, but rather may reflect a 
limited  understanding  of  the  wri tten  system  being  translated  with  the 
resultant blind-spots in the final translation. Some have argued that trans-
lations  “invariably  skew  the  perceived  meanings  of  the  target  language 
towards  the  translator’s  values  and   prejudices”  (Gladston  2016,  p. 3) 
which may or may not be a conscious bias. Gladston (2016) further con-
tends that the meaning loss in translations could be a deliberate choice 
involving “asymmetrical power relations that re nder the values of subal-
tern cultures marginal if not invisible to their dominant others” (Gladston 
2016,  p. 3). In  the  culture  of  the  target  language,  if  Chinese  concepts 
represented in hanzi are not understood or valued, it is likely that the cul-
tur al concepts will ‘disappear’ in translation. Instead, concepts within the 
target language, for example, European terms, will replace them. Thus, 
translation can contribute to the misunderstanding between cultures. summAtIon

This book has explored the theoretical basis of the concept of culture from 
the perspective of Chinese cultural theories and theorising; p ractically it 
has  drawn  on  research  with  Chinese  background  teachers  who  were 
Chinese language teachers in a cross-cultural setting that wa s Australian 
schools. By implementing key Chinese cultural concepts and theorisation, 
the research examined the participant group’s ‘Chineseness’—as Chinese 
people, Chine se language teachers and Chinese language teachers abroad. The investigation into the influence of these teachers’ ‘Chineseness’, and 
their Chinese culture, revealed how and why these teachers were moti-
vate d towards any kind of cultural shift during their experiences of cross- 
cultural teaching, learning and living. Whilst undertaking the research it became clear that to understand the 
role Chinese culture was playing in the Chinese language teachers’ daily 
