"What methodological foundation does Chinese culture theorising rely on, and in what ways does this contrast with the approach typically associated with Western knowledge production?","CHAPTER 6

Conclusion: Characteristics of Chinese 

Culture

Abstract  This book commenced from the standpoint of Western domi-
nant theorising in cul tural studies, to provide a brief review of what has 
been defined as ‘culture’. It continued with an exploration of theories and 
theorising of culture from the Chinese perspective. Chinese theorisation 
of culture was examined through analysing a group of Chinese teachers’ 
perceptions  and  experiences  of  teaching  Chinese  la nguage  in  a  cross- 
cultural setting and an analysis of two key government documents. The 
reciprocated examination between theories and research data enables this 
chapter  to  summarise  and  list  some  key  characteristics  of  the   core  of 
Chinese culture: it is rooted in its written language (hanzi) which makes 
Chinese language teaching inseparable from the culture; the core  of the 
culture  is  closely  linked  back  to,  streamlined  with  and  continues  from 
China’s elongated history; most importantly Chinese culture  education is 
systematically built into the national education system across sectors and 
across history. Keywords  National culture • Essential • Language root • 
Untranslatability • Chineseness • Commonality

