"Why did a nutrition initiative aimed at pregnant mothers, which was successful in Tamil Nadu, fail to achieve similar outcomes in Bangladesh even though the two settings seemed comparable?","The  point  is  often  made  about 
phonics-based reading tests that involve children sounding out made-up 
‘words’: that this is hardly what we mean  by reading (see Davis  , 2012). Similarly  learning  to  apply  mathematical  techniques  competently  may 
not be the same as acquiring mathematical understanding (and may even 
sta nd in the way of it). In a recent book on  Evidence-based Policy , Cartwright and Hardy note 
that RCTs cannot in fact tell us ‘what works’: they can only tell us that a 
p articular policy worked in a particular time in a particular school, hos-
pital or other setting. To go further than that to ‘it works generally’ we 
have to be clear just what constitute relevantly similar schools or hospi-
tals and so on, and thi s requires judgement   or, as Cartwright and Hardy 
usually call it, deliberation. They give the vivid example of how a nutri-
tion project in Tamil Nadu especially designed to teach pregnant moth-
ers to nourish themselves and their  children did not work in Bangladesh, 
even though the contexts appeared very similar. It emerged that in rural 
Bangladesh the mothers did not always have control over what food was 
bought, since there it was the men who went to the ma rket, nor how it 
was cooked and distributed, since a signii cant minority of women lived 
with  their  mothers-in-law  and  deferred  to  them. Deliberation  is  also 
needed to answer crucial questions about whether what is identii ed as 
‘working’  has  a  causal  role,  for  instance  to  e stablish  whether  the  high 
standards of reading in a particular school are due to a phonics policy 
or – for example – to teachers who, strongly mo tivated by a sense of just-
ice, are determined that no child shall leave their school without being 
a competent reader, so that they would have made  almost any approach 
to reading ‘work’. It is needed to identify support factors: what ‘works’ 
in a school that can afford supplementary material for a reading scheme 

