on to my fingers, which type very fast, sometime s faster than my thoughts. Luckily 
I know touch typing, so that I don’t waste any time looking for the right keys. To 
me it feels like my typing fingers are connected to my brain, which barely manages 
to give a command and the words are already formulated on the  screen. This, then, 
is  how  I  interpret  my  behavior  as  a  writer  in  my  writing  workspace,  a  space  in 
which the computer is the main means of wr iting. In effect, as Vygotsky puts it, 
the computer is perceived as a psychological tool that is comparable to a tangible 
instrument which serves as a medi ator between the human hand and the object of 
action (Zellermayer & Kozulin, 2004). In my case, the computer as a psychological 
tool dominates my behavioral and cognitive processes as a writer and transforms 
my inner psychological b ehavior into higher mental functions in my writing. The 
computerized writing space is an inseparable part of my essence as a writer and as a 
literate individual in the twenty-first century (Digital Li teracy & Citizenship, 2009). At  first  I  considered  asking  my  colleagues  the  academic  writers  to  attach  a 
