"Which government ministry was responsible for creating the funding instrument behind Germany's ""clusters of excellence,"" and what competitive outcome was it designed to foster among universities?","R&D  departments). Prototypical  examples  are  the  German  so-named 
“clusters  of  excellence”  which  are  funded  through  the  Excellence  Initiative  
(http://www .excellence-initiative.com/), an instrument established in 2005/2006 by 
the Federal Ministry of Education and Research to cultivate competitive profi les 
among  German  universities. These  clusters  typically  involve  university  and  non-
university institutions as well as industry partners – and for that reason are boundary-
sp anning organisations of the contemporary research university (Parker & Crona, 
2012), operating not only within higher education, but also beyond. However, such 
collaborative  research  across  boundaries  is  seriously  challenging,  particularly  if 
the relation is temporary and/or highly dyn amic (De Rond & Bouchiki, 2004). As 
single-source case studies have shown (cf. Deeg & Dufner, 2010; Deeg, 2012), at 
the beginning these can be associated with low initial levels of inter-dependence 
among most network members an d therefore relatively low levels of readiness for 
shared governance and collaboration. Also, these forms of collective action are more 
imposed than elective – and thus serve as a unique example of the areas of tension 
currently permeat ing academic research – particularly as the increasing attempts to 
organise it formally arise more from externally formulated imperatives than from 
 intrinsic  needs. Nevertheless,  the  specific  characteristics  of  networks  provide  for 
unintended, unplanned processes and effects as well. Consequently, these emergent 
patterns contrast or even conflict with deliberate design and thus, as models, even 
these  quite  sophisticated  networ k  structures  do  not  represent  a  sure  formula  for 
success (cf. Deeg, 2012). 203

