of cooperative relations – some of them novel in their character. Given this field of experimentation 
in combination with persistent institutional tradition (cf. Manhart, 2014), Germany forms a unique 
case to study. 3  Notably, similar efforts can be observed in U.K. higher education (cf. Li et al., 2014). 4  Historically, traditional artisanal production can be characterised as a “tranquil”, local and limited 
form  of  production,  whereas  modern  ind ustrial  production  is  in  contrast  a  large-scale,  distributed 
and  more  vibrant  activity. In  the  context  of  this  chapter  the  juxtaposition  denotes  the  shift  from 
interpersonal, collegial to inter-organisational/-institutional, m anagerial collaboration as indicated in 
the title of the chapter. 5  The proposed typology is inspired by a similar one which is outlined in Franz (2013). However, both 

differ in the overall purpose and the naming of constituent elements. 6  Unfortunately, examples of discontinuation are hardly ever reported in the relevant literature. While 
