"Why might adaptive responses in collaborative partnerships prove unsustainable over time, and what ultimate outcome do they risk?","ble 
for and retaining control over almost all important issues, not least taking all strategic 
decisions (Deeg, 2012, p. 10). As a consequence thereof, individual participants are 
subject to collective governance, exerting direct and indirect control over activities. In  the  case  at  hand,  the  network  management  of  the  research  cluster  prompted 
individual scientists to adjust their own research objective s to follow the cluster’s 
strategies and goals as a reaction to initially low levels of readiness for governance 
and  collaboration  amongst  networ k  members. This  adaptation  via  means  of 
management is evident, taking into consideration the reciprocity being characteristic 
of networks (mutuality) and t he limited autonomy of single actors being embedded 
in complex webs of relations (individuality). Taken  together,  both  examples  might  suggest  that  adaptive  reactions  are  most 
appropriate for the conditions in which HEIs usually operate. Yet, the decisive factor 
for such a reaction is rather a basic inequality (difference in size or imbalance in 
power) or unilateral or mutual depende nce in the relationship. Also adaptation is 
less favourable in the long run. As subliminal tensions often continue, this reaction 
is not always sustainable and finally prone to result in discontinuation Nevertheless, 
in some  cases it is a necessity. Hence overall, alternation and reorientation seem to 
be more advantageous for both the individual institution and the partnership. Lastly, 
discontinuation6  offers  some  unsuspected  advantages  in  the  aftermath,  because, 
when ultimately taken, it can result in strengthened  self-confidence and benefits for 
the individual institutional profile. Reconsidering briefly the examples given in the 
previous section, the recent formation of strategic partnership resonates with the idea 
of reorienta tion or relaunch. However, in the case reported it does not seem to be 
motivated by the need to overcome controversies or to rekindle a dormant relation. Hence the typology is more suited as a decision matrix for individual (managerial) 
reactions in existing partnerships, while not being capable of tri ggering any proactive 
behaviour. The  reason  for  this  is  mainly  the  underlying  transactional  (“give  and 
take”) orientation of the typology, in contrast to a transformational  orientation which 
is a value-based approach to create enhanced relationships.7

