organisations create and manage an entire portfolio of different/diverse relationships. 201

J. DEEG

Additionally, the isolated approaches have fostered an “entitative” view, cumulating 
the properties of independent entities rather tha n reaching a wholesale, relational 
perspective (cf. Dyer & Singh, 1998). But the relational space between cooperating 
organisations is an object of study worth pursuing on its own terms. Notwithstanding the fact that there is an abundant stream of literature on IORs, 
context-specific  systematisation  of  higher  education  is  compar atively  rare. This 
being the case, it seems worthwhile to propose an analytical tool to classify existent 
forms of academic cooperation. A key way of organising them into coherent accounts 
is by means of typologies (cf. Fiss, 2011). Usually these are based on a number of 
distinct dimensions, and in the present chapter the following three basic dimensions 
are proposed: as a first  quite natural dimension, the number of partners or parties 
is set up, varying between bi- or tri-/multilateral relations. Then the nature of the 
relationship forms a second dimension which is represented by a general, specific or 
selective purpose. Thirdly, the scope of cooperation can be taken into consideration 
too: namely, whether it operates within or expands beyond higher education (e.g. collaborating with industrial partners). Together these dimensions constitute a basic, 
poly-dimensional framework for classifying actual cases of cooperation:

Figure 1. A tentative typology of inter-institutional cooperation  

in higher education

