"In the murder mystery activity used as a learning tool in a school library, how do the alibis given by the six suspects connect to the educational content students are expected to absorb?","We  may  decide  we  need  a  game  that  is  more  bespoke  to  the  learning
outcomes we wish to achieve. It is easy to make our own live action role play
games inexpensively with the same degree of fun in play and underpinning
of  knowledge  as  enjoyed   in  commercial  ones. Home-made  games  allow
creators more flexibility in the design – we can tailor the challenges to fit into
a required time frame and focus them on def ined learning objectives:

As part of induction the class walk with me around the library and I point out
resources and talk about the rules and how t o use the equipment. Then we have
a scenario called Murder in the Library. To solve the clues [students] need to
have been listening on the walking tour. I suppose I am using the game as a form
of assessment [of] learning. Independent school, England, 11–12 year olds

The plot for this game centres on a murder in the library. There are six suspects. Each suspect has a name that relates to a character in a fiction book. The suspects
have been interviewed and have given short statements, each with an alibi. These
are presented to the students on a quiz sheet. The alibis all relate to rules of the
library, resources, etc., for example, ‘I spent a delightful afternoon in the library
looking at the magazines. I was fascinated by an article in History Today. The
librarian will remember me. She stopped me trying to take the magazine out of
the library – I forgot that magazines can’t be taken out.’ This is followed by
asking questions such  as ‘where are the magazines shelved?’ and ‘can magazines

