which are achievable and relevant to learning outcomes. We need to consider
how  we  give  feedback  and  make  assessments  throughout  the  learning
process. Will a live game approach prove beneficial to students in our library
environment? Let us consider some games that place students at the centre
of learning so their experience drives the game. Games involving movement as a motivator for engagement and
cognition
Physical  movement  can  be  a  great  energiser,  which  in  turn  increases
con centration and raises the level of engagement. Games that tap into this
phenomenon can be cost free and a great way to start a lesson. One possibility
is to prepare a class for thinking about book titles in a particular genre or
maybe a vocabulary that is used within a library, or wor ds they associate with
information literacy, with a pairing game:

We used this game to think about academic honesty and what it means before
introduc ing some project research. One student stands facing their partner with
palms turned upwards and the other ‘gives’ them a word, speaking the word
and placing their downturned ha nds on the upturned palms. They then swap
over, and both having received their words move on to someone else. At the end
of a pre-set time, they return to tables and write down as many words as they
can remember as a small group. Music can be played to give clues to the time
allowed, adding another sensory aspect to the game. Independent school, England, 16–17 year olds

Using music as an energiser or a timer can add a new dimension to the game,
or we can use it as part of  the scaffolding. Many successful action games can
be based on party games we may have played as children ourselves:

I set up a game called musical bookcases to encour age students to find more
books on the library shelves. The class moves around the library while the music
plays but when it stops, they have to visit the nearest bookcase and find
something that catches th eir attention. Middle school, USA, 12–14 year olds

