This game would take time to design and ensure that all the elements are in
place, but it enables students to practise the ta sks they require to use the
school library in the future. However, we need to consider the practicalities. What if we were to have a series of induction sessions throughout the day? Would it be possible to run this type of game? We should balance game play
and learning outcomes and be careful that the reward and the puzzle solving
do not override the educational purpose of the  game. Will it detract from
longer term retention if challenges do not relate to learning objectives? We
need to consider how we might measure the success of the game so we can
reflect further on these questions and adapt the design if necessary. Problem-solving games using a locked chest or breakout room are more
sophisticated than games involving quests and puzzles, but take even more
time an d materials to run effectively. We have already seen how games using
a locked box or a digital presentation can engage students, and now we
consider these games as a whole room space . Detwiler, Jacobson and O’Brien
(2018) suggest that breakout room exercises mimic the emotional and enquiry
mindset of the research process because the  tasks involved are not linear and
often require a degree of trial and error before a solution is found to the set
challenge. Rouse  (2017)  points  out  the  advantages  of  learning  in  an
environment that encourages collaboration, critical thinking, patience and
resilienc e. The success of locked chest or breakout games pivots on good
design elements. We might wish to think about:

■ how long the game elements take to plan
■ how much expenditure and ongoing budget is needed 
■ how long the game take s to set up and if it has to be reset or checked

each time it is played

