phase of PECS, the child will be able to speak some words (Zhou and Cheng 2016). PECS is effective in enhancing functional communication skills of children with 
ASD  and  does  not  hinder  verbal  production,  although  an  agree ment  is  yet  to  be 
reached regarding the relationship between the two. The results of PECS training 
revealed  that  the  intention  to  initiate  communication  conduces  to  overcoming 
speaking difficulties (Xu 2014). In terms of the factors influencing the intervention 
effects of PECS, Wang held that the effects differ among individuals as they have 
different cha racteristics, especially the ability of gesture imitation before interven-
tion (Wang 2014). PECS has clear visual indications and is easy to carry and use, 
but the user should be able to distinguish picture cards and take into account his or  
her writing speed when using the system (You 2015). PECS intervention has certain impacts on children with ASD. However, the lack 
of standardized evaluation tools to examine the maintenance and generalization of 
intervention  effects  renders  the  results  un reliable  enough. Evaluations  should  be 
conducted with more reliable and valid tools (You 2015). The effects of PECS are compared with that of other aided communication sys-
tems including sign language and speech-generating devices (SGD). When compar-
ing the effects of PECS and sign language training, it is found that the former is 
more effective for children with ASD without the abil ity to imitate a set of complex 
hand  gestures,  while  the  latter  works  best  for  those  with  such  ability,  if  in  the 
absence of a better  method. According to Wang, individuals who received sign lan-
guage  intervention  produced  higher  proportions  of  speeches. The  intervention 
effects differ among individuals as they have different characteristics, especially the 
ability of gesture imitation before interv ention. After PECS intervention, adults tend 
to acquire sign language more quickly and are more capable of using it to commu-
nicate with different people in  different environments. This is perhaps a result of 
