13. DISSONANCE IN PARTICIPANT VOICE

Research Strategies That Harmonise Distortions in Participant Data

INTRODUCTION

The production of trustworthy resea rch is an essential goal for researchers to aspire
to, regardless of whether a quantitative or a qualitative approach is adopted as the
mode  of  inqu iry. While  trustworthiness  as  a  concept  has  customarily  been
associated with the qualitative tradition (Shenton, 2004), the notion of producing
rese arch  that  can  be  trusted  is  also  fundamental  to  quantitative  inquiry. The
Committee  on  Science,  Engineering,  and  Public  Policy  (1995)  asserted  that  “the
scientific  research  enterprise  is  built  on  a  found ation  of  trust”,  and  that  “society
trusts the results of research to reflect an honest attempt by researchers to describe
the  world  accurately   and  without  bias”  (p.  R5). Dubious  research  may  diminish
public  trust  in  research  and  researchers,  and  also  mislead  other  researchers  to
pursue fruitless research  agendas that serve only to waste resources. To  preserve  the  opinion  of  and  value  for  research,  ethical  standards  and
methodological  guidelines  have  been  established  to  guide  in vestigators  in  their
endeavour  to  produce  trustworthy  research. A  discussion  of  these  standards  and
guidelines  is  beyond  the  scope  of  this  chapter;  however,  one  aspect  of  research
