Perceptions of Writing

This matter is precious to me; it’s very important to me. You inject your inner world, and you connect to something spiritual. The  writers  consider  writing  meaningful  and  important  to  them  as  professionals 
working in the academic world. “Writing is a requirement and is also integral to my 
personal  fulfillment,  certainly  as  someone  whose  profession,  which  is  inseparable 
from   my  personal  life,  involves  collecting,  organizing,  developing  and  critiquing 
information.”

Most of the writers see writing as a creative a rtistic process and often compare it to 
painting or playing the piano. Discourse analysis of the descriptors (both adjectives 
and nouns) used by the interviewees reveals that they see their writing as essentially 
experi ential  and  fascinating. Discourse  analysis  of  the  figurative  language  in  the 
narratives and of the use of hand gestures points to further perceptions of writing as 
a  physical as well as a metaphysical and spiritual process. WRITING AS ARTISTIC CREATION

When I paint, the painting itself must be understandable to the observer … the 
same thing is true of academic writing. Most of the interviewees consider writing to be artistic work, and indeed many of 
them compare writing to artistic creation. The underlying assumption is that writing 
involves ongoing creativity. “It’s as if you are contributing something to the world 
of knowledge, and not just because you have to.” The timeless dimension of writing 
finds exp ression in the shift to the public sphere. “It [the writing] no longer stays 
with you. It’s out there, in public.”

Many  of  the  interviewees  see  writing  as  a  form  of  coping,  as  a  riddle  whose 
solution  becomes  clearer  as   meaning  is  created. “It  makes  sense,”  one  of  the 
interviewees commented. The notion of making sense, of becoming understandable, 
reflects  a  dialectic  in  which  writing  inculcates  the  writers  with  meaning  and  the  
writers in turn seek to make the writing meaningful. The  writers  focus  on  the  writing  process,  as  can  be  seen  in  the  following 
comparison one of the interviewees makes between writing and p ainting, which she 
herself also engages in:

