How do writers describe the interplay between the social and personal dimensions of the writing experience?,"It is interesting and enjoyable, and 
it is also present, stirring and liberating. Writing is a source of strength and is thus 
associated with strength and might. Indeed, writing itself is empowering. The process 
is fluent, developing and systematic, on the one hand a catharsis while on the other 
serving as an inner journey. Nevertheless, when it comes to academic writing the 
process is seen as not easy and even difficult and sometimes as a long and Sisyphean 
experience  involving grunt work. The descriptors used to describe the process and product of writing reveal another 
aspect of the writers’ perceptions of writing—the social communica tive aspect. This 
can be seen in descriptions such as it’s a social matter, a human place, something 
that is personal and participative, something communicative  as well as inviting. On 
the other hand, the social nature of writing paradoxically allows for the personal 
component, for according to some of the writers writing involv es a great deal of 
intimacy. The experience is so fascinating and treasured that during the interviews many 
