spoken word and rhyming-based series for third- through fifth-grade students; and 
ABC Poetry, an acrostic and  alphabet poetry series for kindergarten through second-
grade students. I owe the most important turning point in my career to the tutelage 
and support at LASC, and I continue to reflect on my experience there as I contin ue 
on in my career as an educator. CREATIVE TEACHING AND CULTURALLY RESPONSIVE EDUCATION

Teaching  and  coordinating  in  creative  spaces  fed  my  desire  to  work  with  varied 
sub ject areas, provide a space for unlimited creativity, serve struggling communities, 

33

H. D. JOHNSON

and make learning exciting and fun. I define a creative classroom as the embodiment 
of infinite possibilities where math, art, science, history, writing, music, and more 
are discovered , not taught through textbook answers and test keys; where questions 
are  more  important  than  quick  answers;  and  where  individuality  is  valu ed  over 
synchronized  group  learning. The  creative  classroom  fosters  individuals,  not 
machine-like clones, who produce outcomes that are both original and of value to 
humanity. In this light, creative instruction requires a guide, one who travels with 
the student. In this effort to guide students, an educator needs to practice culturally responsive 
instruction  (CRI). I  was  first  introduced  to  CRI  when  I  worked  at  University  of 
Kentucky’s Center for Literacy Development. My responsibilities included reading 
