teaching creatively involves unique and independent discovery, while teaching to 
an exam requires memorization and regurgita tion. In relation to my writing class, 
for example, the contradictory message would play out as follows: “Write your own 
unique story, but memorize the be ginning of The Great Gatsby and then write it 
exactly how it appears in the text, ‘In my younger and more vulnerable years…’”

As a writer and creati ve teacher, these lesson models made little sense to me. I  remember  feeling  nudged  into  insecurity,  believing  my  lack  of  a  degree  in 
education made me unfit to understand and teach such models. In addition to my 
insecurity about the proposed lesson structure, after 13 substitute teachers in a six-
month period (with 13 different teaching sty les or no teaching at all), my eighth-
grade students were tired and confused, sitting on the floor and uninspired by school. Most lacked basic writing skills, from developing an idea to elementary grammar 
and mechanics. Furthermore, before my arrival at New Start the school implemented 
a program into writing class in order to help develop cultural connections. After 
teaching the program for a few weeks, I noticed how it sacrificed the integrity of the 
structure and culture that I was attempting to create i n my classroom. Additionally, the program facilitator completely misunderstood the demographics 
of my students. He believed that more than 60 percent of my students were African 
American. After observation, I found that more than 98 percent of my students were 
Latino, not African American. The content for the program, however, was based on 
the South African rebellions of the 1960s, the Civil Rights Movement, and Black 
Arts  Movement—wi thout  any  connection  to  Caribbean  and  Latino  peoples  or 
movements of culture, arts, and society. The school tried to implement a music program, but students were disengaged 
right  from  the  start. The  facilitator  could  not  understand  why  his  efforts  were 
not  recognized  or  understood  by  the  students. He  thought  the  content  would  be 
interesting enough; however, there is more to CRI than simply dropping in some 
minority-related content. The purpose of the music program was again to increase 
cultural understanding, yet the teacher struggled to make a cultural connection with 
the stud ents in my class. I confronted the principal about the program after a few 
