Why does Blackmore (2009) argue that social justice must be at the core of leadership preparation for educators?,"School leaders can learn to be culturally responsive and demonstrate 
social  justice,  ethics,  and  equity  in  schools  for American  society  to   continue  to 
succeed in the 21st century global society. The educational leaders’ commitment to 
minimizing the gaps in achievement rely on the development and implementation of 
policies and practices that  provide stakeholders an equitable learning environment for 
every student. Blackmore (2009) concludes, “Social justice is central to leadership 
preparation because without addressing issues of difference and inequality then  there 
will  be  no  substantial  improvement  of  student  learning  for  those  in  ‘challenging 
circumstances’” (p. 8). Educational  leaders  also  need  to  be  cognizant  of  the  politics  impacting  the 
development  and  dissemination  of  curriculum  and  knowledg e. In  order  for 
transformation to begin, courageous conversations about the impact of race, culture, 
and  social  justice  within  organizational  st ructures  are  necessary  to  alleviate  the 
inequities in the educational setting. Institutions of higher education can empower 
educational leaders to recognize the organizational obstacles that may prove to be 
barriers to the impl ementation of culturally responsive leadership. Doctoral programs 
facilitate developing the competences required to promote and replace traditional 
structures  with  more  equitable  structures  t o  meet  the  needs  of  all  stakeholders. Educational leadership programs provide administrators with the skills necessary to 
identify the dynamics of the school center, including school facu lty and staff, along 
with student backgrounds, which have significant influence upon learning (Noguera 
& Wing, 2006; Villegas & Lucas, 2002). These skills include solving problems of 
practice, addressing diversity, and creating change within schools. 56

