The professorial interviews and survey responses of a cohort at this South Florida 
university  helped  to  shed  light   on  the  perceptions  of  social  justice  and  cultural 
responsiveness  in  the  university’s  Ed.D. curriculum. It  is  noteworthy  that  not  all 
possible participants responded, and one person requested to be interviewed was 
unavailable for a phone interview  or written correspondence. Future research needs 
to explore the perceptions of additional cohorts at this university to ascertain if there 
are overlaps in the patterns, themes , and discrepancies. Additional faculty can also 
be interviewed to determine if the patterns, themes, and discrepancies are aligned 
with the faculty who participated. The responses from professors and doctoral students emphasized the preference 
of the students in determining the depth of particular topics. The professors embraced 
a constructivist theory of teaching educational leadership; this does not necessarily 
assure that sensitive, but critically  relevant topics such as cultural responsiveness, 
equity,  and  social  justice  are  addressed. The  professors  worked  to  guide  the 
conversations,  but  ultimately  relied  on  student  interest  and  passion  to  guide  the 
