 Sociomaterial Orders in the Classroom: Theoretical 
Perspectives from the Sociology of Knowledge 
and Actor-Network Theory

Generally, analyzing  the interactions of teachers and students with learning mate-
rials remains a challenge for subject-related empirical classroom research. The tea-
cher and the students use manifold objects, such as textbooks, worksheets or pic-

100

tures presented with a projector, as part of the tea ching and learning process. In 
science education, frequently used subject-specific objects are equipment for expe-
riments, original natural objects or models (cf. Roehl, 2013). Materiality is there-
fore highly relevant to the construction of knowledge and meaning-making proces-
ses (cf. Fühner and Heinicke, Gómez Thews and Menthe in this volume). The combination of the sociology of knowledge (Mannheim, 1952) with actor- 
network theory, and here especially with the ethnographic work of Latour ( 1999), 
has proven to be particularly fruitful for examining the social function of material 
objects used in the classroom. Preliminary work can be found in Asbrand et al. (2013) and Martens et al. (2015) on the question of how the interactions of human 
and nonhuman agents in the classroom can be described theoretically and recorded 
methodicall y in an appropriate manner within the framework of documentary vi-
deo analysis. This approach allows us to explore the objects’ function in teaching 
and learning processes and in establishing and reproducing the social orders of  the 
classroom. Mannheim (1982, p. 187) coined the term “contagion” to describe the 
existential connection between people and things (objects, artifacts) that fosters a 
deep understan ding. In epistemological and sociological terms, Latour also strength-
ens the links between human and nonhuman agents, which he describes in their 
differe nt forms and qualities as creating new possibilities for action and knowledge 
acquisition. Latour described various forms of associations between human and 
nonhuman agents, which are also relevant to classroom interaction (i.e., interfe-
