"What is the goal of the documentary method regarding the perspectives of the individuals being studied, and what principle guides this process?","The value of the documentary-method approach for classroom interaction re-

search on science teaching and learning is briefly explained in two points :

First, the analytical approach to communicative and conjunctive knowledge en-
ables us to focus on the relevant relationship between explicit scien tific, didactic, 

102

pedagogic or societal norms on the one hand and the habitus of the participants 
resp. the  implicit  structures  of  practice  in  the  classroom  on  the  other  hand 
(Bohnsack, 2014). Therefore, research using the documentary method can answer 
the  following  questions: Which  scientific,  didactic  and/or  pedagogical  norms  of 
 teaching are articulated as explicit knowledge, and how are they related? Which 
implicit routines and inherent logics does teaching and learning practice follow? How are these routines related to classroom interaction? What possibilities for learn-
ing and knowledge acquisition arise against the background of a specifically struc-
tured  teaching  approach? This  distinction  between  explicit  communicative  and 
implicit conjunctive knowledge is important to detect whether they are congruent 
or not. Specific norms may be explicitly articulated without being part of the impli-
cit routines underlying the interactions. Second, the documentary method aims at reconstructing the perspective of the 
subjects under investigation without evaluation according to external no rms (i.e., 
“bracketing the validity aspect”, Bohnsack, 2010, p. 109). Thus, the method fol-
lows the principle of methodically controlled perspective taking (Bohnsack, 2005). However, the question of whether biological knowledge constructed in the class-
room is consistent with scientific norms, such as evolutionary theorie s, and whether 
the teacher’s actions correspond to professional norms is vital for science educa-
tion research. In the empirical analyses, the question is asked what function the 
articulation of specific subject matter has in classroom interaction. In other words, 
the analyses focus on the norms the participants reproduce and make relevant. In a 
subsequent step, these results of the empirical reconstruction can be discussed in 
view of the researcher’s scientific norms and scientific com mon sense. 3.2 

