"What is identified as central to the sport-specific concept of elite-Bildung, and how does this concept describe the relationship between practising and the transformation of one's movement capacity?","Other approaches related to Bildung highlight the transformation of the self- 
world relation. Drawing on the German conception of experience (Erfahrung) as 
involving negativity (Gadamer 2004) this line of research has highlighted the con-
stru ctive  role  of  encountering  otherness,  e.g.,  something  unfamiliar,  surprising, 
disturbing, challenging, or unexpected that transcends one’s ho rizon. It is central to 
the sport-specific concept of elite-Bildung (Aggerholm 2015) and the concept of 
existential learning in sport (Ronkainen et al. 2020). These conceptions draw on 
Bildung theory to provide non-instrumental and critical alternatives to accounts of 

34

K. Aggerholm and M. Giese

skill   acquisition  in  talent  development  and  life  skills  in  sport. Central  to  elite- 
Bildung  is  transformation  through  practising,  which  entails  repetitions  of  diffe-
rence. Practising movements that you are not yet able to do can describe a form of 
negativity that can broaden one’s movement horizon. This can describe the process 
of Bildung in physical education and other movement cultures as well (Aggerholm 
2021; Aggerholm et al. 2018). It is, for example, evident in parkour where practiti-
oners seek challenges in the environment and practise to transform and refine their 
bodily hab its (Aggerholm and Højbjerre Larsen 2017). Another way of transcen-
ding one’s horizon can occur in nature (Gurholt 2008). This has been analysed as 
related to Bildung in the experience of children encountering otherness in outdoor 
activities (Sanderud et al. 2019). Concerning the critical-constructive account of Bildung, developed by Klafki 
with inspiration from critical theory, it is possible to see similar aim s in approaches 
informed by transformative pedagogy. There has been a growing interest in this 
approach to physical education. In part five of the Routledge Handbook of Physical 
Education Pedagogies (Ennis 2017), seven chapters were devoted to transforma-
tive pedagogies. Mikael Quennerstedt (2019) has presented examples of transfor-
mative learning that he describes as genuinely educative, which we in the present 
cont ext will interpret as Bildung-related. They include the work of Kim Oliver and 
colleagues  to  create  more  equitable  physical  education  by  supporting  girls  to 
