"According to Benner, why is the concept of negativity considered indispensable to understanding the process of Bildung?","r Pädago-
gik) towards a critical-constructive approach. As a corrective to classic accounts of 
Bildung, critical theorists such as Habermas, Adorno and Horkheimer argued that 
Bildung  needs  to  include   critical  components  related  to  the  social  and  political 
forms of power (see Bünger and Mayer 2018). This informed Klafki’s account of 
Bildung as an “ability for reasonable self-determination, which presupposes or in-
cludes emancipation from externa l determination, as an ability for autonomy, for 
freedom  of  one’s  own  thinking  and  one’s  own  moral  decisions”  (Klafki  2007, 
S. 19). Drawing on classical conceptions of Bildung, he highlighted three basic 
abilities of Bildung in a critical-constructive dealing with a general conten t infor-
med by epoch-typical key problems: the “ability for self-determination”, “ability 
for  co-determination”  and  “ability  for  solidarity”  ( Klafki  2007,  S. 52). In  this 
sense, students should be enabled to make individually meaningful decisions about 
their own lives and social activities (ability for self-d etermination). However, they 
should also be enabled to actively participate in shaping our society (the ability for 
co-determination) and they should develop the a bility to recognize the right of their 
fellow human beings to self- and co-determination (the ability for solidarity). 30

