Following  the  termination  of  the  Second World War  in  1945  the  United  Nations 
 Organisation was established with strong support from the United States and with 
a  headquarters  in  New  York. Many  subordinate  agencies  were  also  established 
including:  (a)  the  International  Court  of  Justice,  (b)  the  International  Atomic 
Energ y Agency, (c) the International Monetary Fund, (d) the World Bank, (e) the 
World Health Organisation, (f) the World Trade Organisation, (g) the Inter national 
Labour Office, (h) the Food and Agriculture Organisation, (i) the United Nations 
International  Children’s  Emergency  Fund  (UNICEF),  and   (j)  the  International 
Development  Organisation. In  1946,  the  United  Nations  Educational  Scientific 
and Cultural Organisation (UNESCO) was founded. It has been this organisation, 
UNESCO, based primarily in Geneva, but with affiliated centres in different parts of 
the World, and at times without  support from the United States has initiated, endorsed 
and  supported  educational  activity,  as  well  as  scientific  and  cultural  programs. UNESCO, particularly in its initial years strongly supported solving the particular 
problems of developing countries. It formed an institute for the training of teachers 
in  Mexico  in  1951,  and  institutes  primarily  for  lifelong  education  in  Hamburg, 
German y in 1951, and for educational planning in Paris in 1963. UNESCO’s fields 
of activities are educational, social, cultural, technological, and political as well as 
economic development, and all six fields in fluence and are heavily influenced by the 
movement of globalisation toward a united World. UNESCO is increasingly seen as 
operating across all stages of human life, from early childhood through schooling, 
technical training, higher educati on in universities and institutes of technology, as 
well as life-long and recurrent educational activities. 243

