2003, p. 18). This program is directed towards the well-being of the more than seven billion 
people  now  living  in  the  approximately  200  countries  and  econ omies  of  the 
World. It is this program initiated and sponsored by UNESCO that has stimulated 
the  assessment  and  evaluation  of  education  in  the  five  countries  u ndertaking  the 
preparation of this book. PISA involves youth at the 15-year-old age level, that can 
be considered to be related to a terminal stage of compulsory schooling as well as the 
tr ansition by many youth from school to work and the beginning of adult life. The 
remarkable contribution made by UNESCO to education during this period has been 
reviewed by Power (2015), and a more detailed account on ‘Achiev ing Education for 
