It can no longer be assumed that the resources available on planet Earth, which is 
the only planet where life is known to exis t, are unlimited following a time when 
there has been remarkable growth in the population of the World over the previous 
two centuries. At the beginning of the nineteenth century there were approximately 
one billion people living in the World. By 1930, the population had doubled to two 
billion  people  and  at  the  present  time,  close  to  the  beginning  of  the  twenty-first 
century t here are more than seven billion people living in the World. By the time 
the samples of students who responded in 2012 to the PISA tests considered in this 
volume are approaching the age of 50 years, it is est imated that there will be nine 
billion people. Chapter 9 in this book reports that in the five countries involved in 
this study, from the middle of the twentieth century in 1950, there is expected  to be a 
four-fold increase in the combined population of the five countries by 2050. Similar 
to the growth in the population of the people living in these five countries there is 
