comparative educators was held at the UNESCO Institute for Edu cation in Hamburg. During that meeting Arnold Anderson from the University of Chicago advanced the 
idea of the need to:

try to conduct strict comparisons between natio nal systems of education using 
empirical measures of resource inputs and student achievement. What he had in 
mind was to conduct a cross-national evaluation of cognitive ‘yield’ of various 
education systems by means of achievement tests admin istered to comparable 
samples of students. (Husén & Tuijnman, 1994, p. 5) 
Starting  with  these  ideas  the  International  Association  for  the  Evaluation 
of  Educational  Achievement  (IEA)  was  led  by  Torsten  H usén  from  Sweden  as 
Chairman, Benjamin Bloom from the University of Chicago and Robert Thorndike 
from Teachers College, Columbia University in th e United States. The founding of 
IEA was not only a significant development in the field of Comparative Education, 
but also gave rise over time to the Programme for  International Student Assessment 
(PISA) by OECD from Paris that currently supplies the framework and structure 
within which the studies reported in  this book are written. Gilbert Peaker (1967), 
who had led and reported on the Plowden National Survey in England and Wales in 
1965, and James Coleman (1971), who had repor ted on the Equality of Educational 
Opportunity  Survey  in  the  United  States  in  1966,  also  greatly  influenced  the 
statistical analyses of t he cross-national sets of data collected initially by IEA and 
subsequently by PISA. 240

