What circumstances in the late 1970s led to the creation of a new type of collaborative meeting format at Lund University?,"ing  of  action  research  in  the  Nordic  countries. The  tradition  of  study 
circles  gave  shape  to  a  distinctive  Nordic  understanding  of  the  relation  between 
developments  in  working  lif e  and  university  knowledge. A  form  of  study  circle, 
called  a  research  circle,  was  developed  at  Lund  University,  one  of  the  oldest 
universities  in  Sweden,  in   the  late  1970s  when  the  union  movements  became 
interested  in  knowledge  production  in  the  universities. At  Lund  University, 
different courses for elected  union  representatives were  started,  in the context of 
which both university lecturers and un ion representatives found there was a need 
for new and more flexible forms of discussion that respected the perspectives the 
different  parties  bro ught  to  the  table. The  research  circle  came  to  be  a  form  for 
meetings and a collaborative way for discussions with a focus on questions decided 
upon by the par ticipants. Issues discussed in research circles are varied – such as 
the working environment in municipalities and regions or development possibilities 
in  dif ferent  kinds  of  businesses  (Holmstrand  &  Härnsten,  2003). Studies  in 
working life expanded during the 1980s and the concept of participatory research 
came  into  favour. Only  in  the  1990s  did  school  development  in  collaboration 
between universities and schools come onto the agenda. Research circles 
