56). In  action  research,  action  can  be  understood  as  affecting  both  the  actors 
(professional  development)  and  the  practices  that  they  ar e  involved  in  by 
developing these practices in a systematic way. In facilitation, the relation between 
acting – reflecting – acting is emphasised. The collaboration in a partnership can be viewed as a meeting or confrontation 
between different fields of knowledge. To convey the idea that knowledge can be 
democratic,  Gustavsson  (2002)  proposes  the  use  the  Aristotelian  division  of 
knowledge where practi cal knowledge is accorded equal value. Such knowledge, 
which  is  possessed  by  people  with  hands-on  experience,  is  identified  and 
characterised as a form of knowledge that is fami liar, tacit or experiential, whilst 
academic knowledge is, by contrast, characterised as propositional knowledge. The 
Aristotelian concepts of episteme as scientific knowledge and poesis and praxis as 
practical knowledge,  forms  a  device  for categorising know ledge into three parts: 
knowing, knowledge (know-how) and practical wisdom. Knowing  (theory)  or  propositional  knowledge  has,  since  Plato,  had  the 
privileged right of interpretation. Theory, in this way, is thus connected to science 
and not to humans. Practical knowledge, on the one hand, is techne, which is as a 
practical  –  productive  form  of  knowledge. It  is  connected  to  both  knowing  and 
creating  within  fields  such  as  handicraft,  art  and  poetry. On  the  other  hand, 
practical knowledge is practical wisdom. It differs from practical handicraft as it is 
carried  out  in  relations  between  people. Our  relations  to  our  fellow  humans  are 
different  than  to  things. Acting  in  social  life  is  for  the  good  itself  and  acting  is 
related to ethics and politics. You can’t be wise without at the same time working 
for the good of humankind. That which we work for is of course always carried out 
