It is designed to be located close to the real world in  order to generate new 
theories  in  which  the  common  objective  is  normative  and  strives  for 
improvements in existing practical situations (T iller 2007, p. 55). Action learning is more directed towards models, concepts and theories that can 
support practitioners and give them  inspiration  for improvement. These common 
interests and meeting places require reciprocity between the parties. Tiller has been 
an important inspiration for Norwegian researchers to work with university study 
programmes  (action  learning)  that  support  teac her-researchers  working  in  their 
own  schools  and  other  educational  settings  in  Norway  and  Sweden,  as  well  as 
stimulating a number of  action research projects in schools (Eilertsen, 2007; Furu, 
2007; Lund, 2007). Noticeable in action research in educational settings in the Nordic countries is 
the  emphasis  on  collaboration  or  partnership  between  universi ties  and  schools. Whether this is based on the tradition of bildung and the development of research 
circles  in  work  life  studies  is  hard  to  say,  but,  given   the  pervasiveness  of  those 
traditions and perspectives, it is probable that they have had some impact on how 
educational  action  research  is  c arried  out  ‘in  a  Nordic  light’. We  think  that 
educational  action  research  in  the  Nordic  countries  has  a  particular  potential  to 
nurture praxis. This potential might be evaluated according to five quality criteria 
developed by the Finnish researchers Heikkinen, Huttunen and Syrjälä for narrati ve 
action research (Gustavsson, 2007). The first criterion is the principle of historical 
continuity which refers to the way in which the history of action is analysed. The 
second, the principle of reflexivity, embraces the relationship of the researcher to 
her or his object, and to her or his presumptions about kno wledge and reality, as 
well  to  the  transparency  of  the  researcher’s  descriptions  of  the  material  and 
methods they study. Thirdly, the principle of dialectics relates to the presentation 
