In what way does action research differ from both the Dutch and Scandinavian models when it comes to how teachers engage with educational change?,"a bureaucracy. Traditionally accepted innovation models fit into these social orders (Havelock, 
1978). In the Dutch situation, there is an emphasis on coercive approaches based 
on  authority,  in  which  changes  are  imposed  upon  teachers  by  other s. In  the 
Scandinavian  situation,  the  emphasis  is  on  re-educational  approaches,  in  which 
teachers  are  persuaded  by  others  of  the  need   for  change. In  action  research, 
however,  teachers  do  not  allow  themselves  to  be  guided  so  much  by  imposed 
changes  or  by  the  strength  of  othe rs’  convictions. They  allow  themselves  to  be 
guided mainly by the professional reality: Are the prevailing assumptions about the 
education  based  on  reality? Has  the  change  really  taken  place  and  is  it  also  an 
improvement? If so, for whom is it an improvement? Are the insights gained about 
how the real situation operates valid and useful? Starting from this position makes 
the teachers into agents in research and change processes. Final remarks: methods in action research 
 
With teachers as agents in research and change processes, action research requires 
the  operation  of  a   number  of  specific  rules  of  the  game  and  the  employment  of 
specific  methods  which  distinguish  illusion  from  reality  in  pedagogisc he  praxis. Desire and conviction are necessary but the narrative – the realised change – also 
has  to  be  right  and  be  described  in  valid  terms. As  explained  earlier,  the  Dutch 
academic  world  mainly  judges  action  research  in  the  context  of  knowledge 
acquisition. Because  of  this  the  envisaged  rules  of  the  game  and  methods  are 
biased  towards  those  of  empirical-analytical  scholarship  with  its   emphasis  on 
rationality. With  regard  to  action  research  methods,  we  are  certainly  thinking  in 
terms  of  strengthening  the  rational  content,  but  not  in  an  e mpirical  or  analytical 
sense. Action  research  belongs  to  the  interpretative  research  tradition  and  this  is 
in  Nurturing  Praxis. why 
