"According to Cunha's 1980 work on education and social development in Brazil, what three directions did the main approaches to education emphasize?","Cunha (1980), in the 
book Education and Social Development in Brazil, presents three directions given 
by the main approaches: (1) investing in educa tion is investing in “human capital”, 
which would contribute to the growth of national income; (2) education is the key to 
building a “modern” socie ty, due to which following “advanced” and “developed” 
nations’ model is necessary; (3) education is the key for building a just society and 
can also  be an instrument to achieving opportunity equalization (Cunha, 1980). Analyzing  Brazilian  education  policies,  we  can  identify  the  adoption  of  these 
three approaches. Depending on the context in which the policy is developed, one 
approach is more emphasized than others. In 2003, when the Workers Party (PT) first took presidential office for the first 
time, it set “Brazil, a country for all” as its policy slogan. In 2011, PT changed the 
slogan to “A rich country is a country without poverty”. In 2015, the slogan was 
changed to “Brazil, educating homeland”. In  general,  PT  governments  have  emphasized  the  role  of  education  for  the 
country’s development and for building a just society without bar riers preventing 
each  citizen  to  develop  their  potential  according  to  their  personal  motivations. At the same time, the country’s development would create the circumstances for 
every citizen to seek more dignified living conditions and contribute  to the smooth 
functioning and growth of economic activity. This understanding shows inspiration from liberal principles, which advocate that 
access to education should not be related to privileged inheritance  or social class, 
but rather at the service of individuals’ development so that they could take their 
place in the social structure by themselves ac cording to their own skills. From this 
perspective, social justice is a parametric value based on the merits accomplished 
by each subject. Cunha discusses some of the reasons why liberal utopia did not take place in Brazil. Whereas individuals depart from extreme unequal conditions and are immersed in a 
state that favors capital concentration in capitalists’ hands, econo mic development 
does  not  guarantee  significant  improvements  in  the  social  sector  or  investments 
that could enable full access and retentio n in public education. The author points 
