(cid:129) Two ﬁctional academic characters with over 40 years combined experience work-
ing in neol iberal tertiary education settings meet up to put the ﬁnishing touches
on the conclusion of a book that they’ve written that focuses on some aspects o f
what it means to work in a neoliberal setting
(cid:129) Their book is a response to one of the key aspects that they think characterizes the
current  neoliberal moment i.e. a proliferation of standardizations (which they also
understand are increasingly prescriptive and rigorously policed)
(cid:129) The book they’ve just  completed responds to this moment via a series of move-
ments—a series of non-standard movements that are arts-based and a little bit
‘sideways’—a ser ies of sideways movements that aim to provoke thinking and
enable difference—a series of non-standard arts-based sideways movements that
are hopeful a nd that are an attempt to make a small contribution to ethical change
(cid:129) The ﬁctional characters have just ﬁnished a book, that following Berla nt (2011)
has it’s eyes open (Sylvester 2019) but that is also moderately hopeful (Berger
2016) and pessimistic
(cid:129) The ﬁctional characters have  both read Morin (2017), Harris and Holman Jones
(2019), and Fawaz (2016) and both like carrots
(cid:129) The ﬁctional characters have been to view a  contemporary dance piece where the
dance involved the eating a bag of carrots which the ﬁctional characters inter-
preted as a commentary on excessive , unnecessary and thoughtless consumption
in capitalist economies
