What considerations do the authors recommend keeping in mind when cellphilm workshops are introduced into classroom pedagogical settings?,"In relation to the cellphilms produced, who owns them—the 
students, or the schools? Can schools claim ownership? If ownership is to be shared, 
can students present or disseminate their work beyond the classroom environment? If so, what are the possible consequences for schools and students, particularly if 
the topics under investigation are socially controversial or cont entious? CONCLUSION

Our participation in a cellphilm workshop provided insights highlighting the value 
of these methods particularly in relation to how it fa cilitates the generation of many 
different voices, perspectives, and truths concerning contentious social issues. It is 
our belief that there is a strong potential for such a workshop format to be introduced 
in  classroom  pedagogical  settings. However,  caution  needs  to  be  exercised; 

131

B. CHAN ET AL. this  includes  adequate  training  for  educators,  addressing  potential  challenges  in 
classroom time management, and developing school policy on  issues pertaining to 
ownership. As a preliminary step towards further developments in cellphilms as a 
pedagogical tool,  researchers  and  schools  might  consider  collaborating in   a  pilot 
study to explore the practicality and viability of introducing cellphilm production 
workshops in different classroom settings. Insights gleaned in this process may serve 
as a basis for the creation of a common framework for schools and educators to 
introduce and facilitate s uch workshops in the future. ACKNOWLEDGEMENT

