What led the group to question whether their entire cellphilm production process was truly participatory?,"hilm collaboratively. Mak (2006) notes that 
participation  in  participatory  video  method  is  subject  to  different  interpretations. For  example,  it  could  refer  to  participants’  involvement  in  the  entire  process  or 
just  segments  of  it  (for  example,  in  shooting  t he  cellphilm),  the  dissemination 

127

B. CHAN ET AL. process during screenings, or as members of an audience. Applying Mak’s multiple 
understandings  of  participation to  our  cellphilm  project,  we  wonder  if  our  entire 
production  process  could  be   considered  to  have  been  participatory. Each  group 
member was involved in most of the production process, but because of time and 
logistical  considerations,  responsibility  for  editing   rested  exclusively  with  one 
individual. Incidentally,  this  was  the  group  member  who  had  reservations  about 
exploring  the  topic  of  harm  reduction. Therefore,  even  though  our  group  had 
discussed and agreed on certain editorial decisions beforehand (such as inserting 
transitions between sequ ences, and creating a dark tone by increasing contrast and 
reducing colour saturations), specific changes made to the cellphilm by the group 
member  ultimately responsible for editing occurred after the workshop. Hence, the 
exact length of transitions, the magnitude of contrast and saturation adjustments, 
all of which may have influenced the characteristics o f the final production, were 
made by a single group member. It could hence be argued that our entire production 
process had not been strictly participatory in nature, since part of the editing step did 
not in volve other group members. Multiple Audiences

The concept of multiple understandings of participations can likewise be applied to 
the audience of our cellphilm. Mitchell and De Lange (2011) argue that screenings 
could be expanded to broader audiences in order to create further opportunities for 
engagement. In our case, there were three distinct audiences, conceived in terms of 
who was viewing it, and where. Both factors, we found, influenced the ways our 
cellphilm production came to be understood and perceived. In the first instance, Claudia and our classmates participated in the screening of 
our cellphilm on the day after our workshop. This occurred in a classroom setting; 
as described earlier, this screening was accompanied by our group’s introduction 
