“SAFE INJECTION AND NEEDLE DISPOSAL SPACES FOR UBC! NOW!”

BRAINSTORMING

Brainstorming  is  fundamental  to  the  cellphilm  production  process,  particularly 
in  relation  to  content  development   (Mitchell,  2011). It  brings  together  diverse 
participants and ideas in search of a consensus on various aspects of the cellphilm 
production, including, but not lim ited to, potential topics to explore, the selection of 
shooting location(s), technical requirements, and the delegation of responsibilities 
during a nd post-production. Our  initial  brainstorming  elicited  a  variety  of  ideas  relating  to  the  prescribed 
prompt of risk on campus. This led to a discussion of issues concerning campus 
safety, student health, gender equality, Indigenous presence, bullying, and others. In 
order to keep track of these ideas, we recorded them on a piece of poster paper (see 
Figure 1). It became clear quickly that even in our small group, perceptions of risk 
on campus (and what required changing) diverged considerably. This divergence, 
on the one hand, resulted in lively discussions that provided valuable insights into 
our individual life experiences and how these  influenced our perceptions of risk. On 
the other, it significantly inhibited our efforts to reach a consensus on our cellphilm 
topic, particularly given the limited amount of time we h ad. Figure 1. Brainstorming and recording ideas

Having considered briefly a range of topics, our group decided, by a majority 
vote, to produce a cellphilm focusin g on safe injection and needle disposal spaces 
on campus. This idea was initially proposed by one group member based on her 
