What two concerns did the invited UBC faculty member raise about the cellphilm's depiction of the actor and the tone of its closing message?,"In the first instance, Claudia and our classmates participated in the screening of 
our cellphilm on the day after our workshop. This occurred in a classroom setting; 
as described earlier, this screening was accompanied by our group’s introduction 
of  our  topic,  a  repeat  s creening,  and  a  subsequent  discussion  and  Q&A  session. This format enabled us to clarify aspects of the cellphilm production that had been 
unclear, and engage the audience in discussions about topics rela ted to drug-use and 
harm reduction. In  the  second  instance,  our  group  had  invited  a  UBC  faculty  member  (who 
was not involved in the course) to view our cellphilm production. This screening 
occurred in a casual setting with our using a laptop while sitting outside a library 
on  campus. We  sought  to  find  out  how  a  public  audience  would  understand  our 
cellphilm production outside the context and space of the workshop. Initially, this 
faculty member understood our cellphilm production as portraying someone needing 
to use the washroom urgently. After her request for a second screening, our message 
relating to drug-use and harm reduction became more apparent. Nevertheless, she 
pointed out several problematic aspects of our cellphilm production. First, our dark 
portrayal of the actor seemed to caricaturize drug-users as the stigmatized Other; no 
doubt, this stemmed from our biases as previou sly discussed. Second, this faculty 

128

“SAFE INJECTION AND NEEDLE DISPOSAL SPACES FOR UBC! NOW!”

member noted that concluding our cellphilm production with the message “SAFE 
INJECTIONS AND  DISPOSALS  FOR  UBC! NOW!”  (see  Figure  3)  postulated 
an activist position that seemed to impose the message on audiences, rather than 
encouraging discussion and enga gement. This may result in the alienation of some 
audience members who may not relate to the need for safe injection and disposal 
spaces. As the faculty member suggested, perhaps audience engagement could have 
been better achieved by our changing the wording into the form of a question,  such 
as “SAFE INJECTIONS AND DISPOSALS FOR UBC?”

Participating in the cellphilm production process and engaging distinct audiences 
in the workshop  (and beyond) produced numerous insights and opportunities for 
us  to  reflect  on  our  preconceptions  about  harm  reduction  and  drug-use. In  this 
sense, we, the cellphilm producers, could be considered to be the third audience. This reflexivity, as MacEntee discovered in her research on gender-based violence 
against young women in South African schools, not only “[elicited]  further insights 
on the topic under investigation but also [supported] participants in understanding 
