"In a learning environment where students are free to select tasks of varying complexity levels, what specific behavioral patterns would indicate a tendency toward overchallenge as opposed to underchallenge?","Randomised Controlled Experiments

13

Abstract
Although many randomised controlled experiments in Education are about
comparing learning outcomes bet ween conditions right after study or practice,
there are experiments that involve repeated measurements as well. This chapter
focusses on experiments that involve series of repeated measurements, some of
which may occur prior to treatment. Besides, since there is evidence that
assessment can itself inﬂuence learning, this chapter also discusses experimental
designs with time series that  allow for testing and estimating the impact of
assessment on learning outcomes at a later time. 13.1 Introduction

in the accuracy of
In recent years, we have seen an increased interest
self-assessment, task selection, and other skills associated  with metacognition and
SRL. Suppose, a group of educational researchers have developed a new simple
checklist that may support self-assessment of learning task performance among
 high school students who are learning geometry. They decide to study the effect of
this checklist on self-assessment, on actual learning task performance, and on the
difference between self-assessme nt and actual
learning task performance. The
rationale behind this interest is as follows. We know that newcomers in a topic are
generally not that good at self-assessing their own performance, and if we want
learners to make their own study  activity choices, inaccurate self-assessment can be
a problem. Underestimation will likely result in too easy learning tasks being
chosen, whereas overestimation can be expected to result in too complex learning
t asks being chosen by a learner. In environments where learners can freely choose
learning tasks from a range of complexity levels and we are able to monitor both
task selection and p erformance on any given task selected, we can quantify

