At what point in the experimental procedure do the participants first have access to the correct answers for the learning tasks?,"Each of the six learning tasks requires the
learner to complete nine steps, each of which is scored as incorrect (0) or correct
(1) resulting in a tas k score on a scale of 0 (minimum) to 9 (maximum). Each of the
steps is of very similar complexity. At the end of each task, learners are asked to
self-assess their performance of the task just completed on that 0–9 scale. The
correct answers are provided only after the completion of the sixth task with
self-assessment. The only thing the researchers still wonder about is how to set up
the different conditions of the experiment, that is: where and how the checklist is 
used. if

13.2 Different Options

13.2 Different Options

197

The researchers have a number of options available. A ﬁrst option is to randomly
allocate the full sample (N) to either of two conditions: one in which participants
receive the checklist all along, and  one in which participants do not receive the
checklist at all. This setup can be useful for instance when effects of checklist use
are small and/or only become visible after some tasks (i.e., condition-by-time
int eraction effect). A second option is to present the checklist to both groups, but for instance in one
group for the ﬁrst three tasks and in the other group for the last  three tasks. This
allows to give all participants the treatment for an equal number of tasks and allows
for studying condition-by-time interaction as well. One type of interaction pattern
that may occur is that in the group where the checklist is presented in the ﬁrst three
tasks, performance is superior  to that in the other group for the ﬁrst three tasks but
no longer for the last three tasks. A third option would combine the ﬁrst two options in a 2-by-2 setup: receive
checklist in the ﬁrst three tasks (yes or no) and receive checklist in th e last three
tasks (yes or no). If the checklist has an immediate positive effect that does not
easily fade, this could result in the group not receiving the checklist at all per-
