"How many degrees of freedom does an ARMA(1,1) model require, and what does each one correspond to?","A stands for autoregressive integrated moving average (e.g.,
Box, Jenkins, & Reinsel, 1994; Brockwell & Davis, 2009; Hannan, 1970; Whittle,

13.3 Eac h Outcome Variable Separately

201

1951). In ARIMA terms, AR1 is ARIMA(1,0,0), and when we add MA1, we obtain
ARIMA(1,0,1). The third parameter in the ARIMA model, which is ‘0’ in ARIMA
(1,0,1) is discussed in Chap. 16 in this book. ARMA(1,1) can be useful for so-called stationary time series: while scores on an
outcome variable of interest may change over time, the way in which i t changes
does not really change (e.g., change more or less following a linear function). For
performance, for example, we see fairly small changes over time (Table 13.1) and
SD does not vary much from task to task. For self-assessment and the difference
between self-assessment and performance, we see some group-speciﬁc changes as a
function of treatment but, like  differences in performance, we can account for those
in the ﬁxed part of our model. In the ARMA(1,1) model, the AR1 states that a score
of individual j in task i is partly a function of the score of that individual j in task
‘i minus  1’, and the MA1 component adds that the score of individual j in task i is
also partly a function of residual in task i and residual in task ‘i minus  1’. In more
complex models, we would go back further in time. For instance, AR2 and MA2
would involve the score (AR2) and error (MA2) in task ‘i minus 2’ as well. However, as discussed in examples in Chaps. 11 and 12, it is in practice frequently
sufﬁcient to just consider the previous task or occasion, meaning there is no need to
go two or even more task s or occasions back. ARMA(1,1) requires only 3 df: one
for AR1, one for MA1, and one for VRES. Table 13.2 presents the estimates of all rRES and VRES in the UN model,
