"What type of learning tasks were created for the experiment, how many steps did each task involve, and what was the total score range assigned to a single completed task?","One would not want to see learners aim
for increasingly complex tasks if the tasks they are choosing are being performed
incorrectly. Likewise, it is undesirable to see learners for increasingly simple tasks
while the tasks they are choosing are performed correctly. The former would be a
tendency towards overchallenge, while the latter is a tendency towards under-
challenge. Both tendencies move away from the concept of zone of proximal
development (see Chap. 1), where learning is expected to take place, and can be
quantiﬁed for the individual learner on an underchallenge-overchallenge dimension
ranging fro m −1 or 100% underchallenge to 1 or 100% overchallenge (i.e., values
closer to 0 being better; Leppink, Paas, Van Gog, and Van Merriënboer, 2019). Besides, another type of undesirable behaviour is to switch between underchallenge
and overchallenge; this kind of ﬂuctuation may indicate either unce rtainty or ran-
dom choice behaviour and can be quantiﬁed for the individual learner into a [0; 1]
variability statistic as well (i.e., values closer  to 0 being better; Leppink et al.,
2019). the researchers wonder

