lenge if learners are given the choice and going back and forth betw een over- and
underestimation may well result in ﬂuctuation on the over- and underchallenge
dimension in a sequence of learning tasks where learners h ave the choice. Inac-
curate self-assessment, whichever direction, is unlikely to result in accurate learning
task selection (i.e., tasks within the learner’s zone of  proximal development). Besides,
the checklist might not only affect
