all of the essays in part ii reflect a more critical direction of my t hinking about 
liberalism. they are steps i took that challenged my original allegiance and led me 
to being of two minds. each focuses on kinds of problems that i conclude (or at 
least imply) liberalism cannot adequately address. in some, the even stronger claim 
surfaces  that  for  these  kinds  of  problems  the  perspective  of  liberalism  actively 
