vocative of reflection, experiment, and re-vision.’ At the  heart of progressive educa-
tion (and indeed, in another way, in traditions of liberal learning), Schwab reminds 
us, is the idea that the scene of l earning itself must feature inquiry. If inquiry is to be 
interwoven with practice, and not a task for a special caste of researchers, this must 
become  part  of  primary  and  secondary   schooling. Given  that  even  in  so-called 
“higher education” we are witnessing a regression to the FedEx model of education, 
content plus delivery, this may  seem a tall order. For Schwab, though, the ‘impos-
sibility’ centers around a simple but profound corollary of this basic premise: that 
teachers themselves must be lear ners and indeed intellectuals. As Schwab (1959, 158–9) explains,

