What approach is recommended to help make the European Standards and Guidelines more comprehensible and easier to put into practice by higher education institutions?,"The  empirical  evidence  from 
Portugal shows that in practice there is no clear correspondence between the ESG 
standards and their implementation g uidelines. In fact, it seems these guidelines – 
which in effect correspond to quality management practices – give rise to different 
constructs from the seven s tandards established by the ESG. This may be a reflection 
of the fact that the guidelines proposed under each standard cover quality assurance 

72

EUROPEAN STANDARDS AND GUIDELINE S FOR INTERNAL QUALITY ASSURANCE

practices  that  are  related  to  several  standards,  despite  being  assigned  to  just  one 
standard in the for mulation of the ESG. We can think of some reasons that may explain the results obtained. One is the 
fact that we are exploring the links between the variables based on the perceptions 
Portuguese  academics  have  of  the  implementation   of  the  ESG. It  may  be  that 
Portuguese  institutions  are  indeed  implementing  individual  guidelines  from  the 
seven standards without paying much attenti on to each standard as a whole. In this 
respect, it is worth remembering the non-prescriptive character of the ESG, i.e. they 
leave room for HEI to implement them within their own organizational and national 
contexts. Another alternative explanation may indeed be the fact that the existing set of 
seven standards do not adequately account for the quality assurance p ractices put 
forward in the ESG Part 1 guidelines. In that sense, our findings may question the 
architecture of the ESG, and consequently the way they are arrived at. Even the new 
version of the ESG (ENQA, 2015) does not resemble the emerging constructs from 
our study. The constructs in this chapter emerge from the perceptions academics 
have  of  their  implementation,  whereas  the  ESG  are  drawn  from  a  smalle r  set  of 
actors in a more top-down approach. We propose that in future the ESG may benefit 
from  drawing  more  on  the  experience  of  those  that  have  to  implement  them  in 
their institu tions. More participation in the drafting of the ESG may facilitate their 
understanding and thus their implementation. We only have data on the Portuguese case, and we cannot compare it to data from 
