of the six extracted factors (see Table 2 and Table A2 in the Appendix). The items 
for each subscale were selected based on their loadings on each one of the extracted 
factors, all loadings being higher than 0.3 (accordin g to Hair et al. (1998); this is the 
threshold value when sample sizes are higher than 350). Whenever an item presented 
loadings higher than 0.3 in more than one factor, the decision was to allocate it to the 
factor where the loading was hig her. Table  2  presents  the  statistics  computed  for  each  one  of  the  subscales. These 
subscales are formed by combining all items loading highly on the factor and using 
their  mean  as  a  replacement  variable  –  the  subscale   (Hair  et  al.,  1998). Internal 
