QR  courses  are  optional  electives,  there  will  be  students   who  may  enrol  for 
questionable  reasons  and may need to be won over to  the  whole  field. They may 
also need to be convinced that QR is not slovenly and undisciplined but should be 
creative scholarship at its best. Arriving  with  a  background  in  quantitative  methods. One  characteristic  that 
several  lecturers  had to  face,  and  students  themselves  had to  overcome,  was  that 
the  latter  often  arrived  wi th  ideas  about  quantitative  approaches  being  the  'only 
way to  the  truth',  well-fixed in  their  minds. In relation  to  this,  Glesne  and Webb 
(1989)  and  Eisenhart  (1989)  have  talked  about  such  students  who  often  want  a 
formula  for  qual itative  research or who  arrive  with a technical  approach  in mind. Fyfe  (1992)  suggests  that  the  mindset  of such  students  needs  to  be  challenged 
because, for instance: 

Observation  is  not only regarded  as inherently easy but also of limited value 
in telling us anything meaningful about the world. Thus one of the challenges 
of teaching  students  about  observation  lies  in  demonstrating  both  the 
complexity  of,  and the  intellectual need  for,  observational fieldwork. (Fyfe, 
1992:  128) 

This was  particularly true  for  students  in the  observed courses  where  they had 
been  trained  in  the  more  scientifica lly-based  disciplines,  such  as  Nursing  and 
