What central contradiction do scholars identify between the academic expectation to teach qualitative research and a widely held belief about how the necessary skills for it are actually acquired?,",  several  writers  have 
perceived  the  teaching  of QR  to  be  particularly  problematic  and,  indeed,  have 
questioned whether it  can be taug ht at  all. Here,  for  example  is  a  statement made 
by  John  Lofland  (1996),  a  very  experienced  QR  lecturer,  researcher  and  writer, 
expressing  how   difficulties  can  continue  to  manifest  themselves,  even  for  'QR 
experts': 

On  and off over some three decades I have tried to  teach under graduate and 
graduate  students  how  to  use  a 
'grounded  theory'  (emergent  analysis) 
approach  to  developing  sociological  analyses  of soci al  interaction  and 
organisation  in  natural settings. I  have not kept statistics but my impression 
is that my rate of success has not been very high. I nonetheless persist in  this 
endeavour,  trying one and then  another strategy or gambit,  hoping I  will hit 
on devices that will work in,  at le ast,  most cases. (Lofland,  1996: 389) 

Echoing this, Clark lets us into a secret: 

I  would like  to  tell you about some of my anxiety about teaching qualitative 
 data  analysis-it's  not for  the first  time  or  even  the  third. I  had taught  it 
several times and even written a chapter in a textbook (Adler & Clark,  1999). But I had never taught it all that well. (Clark & Lang 2002: 348) 

Indeed, Lareau (1986) was even moved to write a paper called:  'Can Qualitative 
Methods  be  Taught?' Similarly,  Schmid  (1992:28)  asks  Is  it possible  to  pursue 
serious experiential and practice-based ethnographic research  within  the confines  
of a conventional university course? Such doubts have occurred in response to the 
paradox  that  lecturers  are  asked  to  teach  QR while  possessing  a  commonly  held 
view  that  'y ou  can  only  learn  it  by  doing'-and,  therefore,  that  the  associated, 
required  skills  cannot be  taught within the  confines  of an  academ ic  course. Stark 
and Watson (1999) have also wondered whether QR,  which requires an emotional 
response, can be taught within academic and  technical structure s. Not surprisingly 
then,  Fetterman  (1987)  referred  to  QR  teaching  as  the  ""unresolved  issue  in 
qualitative methodology"". Such  matters  were  considered  by  Parry,  Atkinson  &  Delamont  (1994:  46)  in 
their  study  of doctoral  social  science  courses. They  reported  that  (particularly 
Anthropology)  departments  shared  a  view  that  Qualitative  Methods  do  not  lend 
