ment of environment via semantic action became a de facto choice: instead of  adapt-
ing somatically to the environment as other species had done, the species homo sapi-
ens opted for exo-somatic evolution (Vygotsky, 1978; Popp er, 1979) (cid:60) an evolution 
which depended not on the body adapting itself physically to the environment but 
on the environment adapting to the  body, underwriting its very obvious weakness-
es, and this adaptation of the environment to the human body was achieved by the 
use of semiotic modali ties. Th  ereaft er the continued evolution of decontextualised 
