Peeler et al., 2012). •  Parents’ comfort with technology. Parents’ role in children’s digital practices is 
likely  influenced  by  their  comfort  with  technology  (Hatzigianni  &  Margetts, 
2014). If parents are comfortable with technology, they may provide more oppor-
tunities for DHNP and may be more likely to engage in them (i.e., provide fee d-
back, play together). •  Parental perceptions of mathematics app effectiveness. Their involvement may 
also extend to their perceptions of children’s digital device use, with some par-
ents having a negative view of children’s dig ital device use more generally (e.g., 
screen time, Delen et al., 2015). In contrast, many parents think that mathematics 
games are beneficial for their children’s mathematics learning (Wartella, 2019); 
