linked  to  educational  institutions  (schools,  universities,  ministries,  textbook  pub-
lishers, etc.). Mathematical modelling is an activity, in which problems from real 
life are solved using mathematical tools. In this activity resides a duality, namely, 
that  (1)  school-based  modelling  activities  offer  a  window  on  the  world  beyond 
school and (2)  the world beyond school offers a window on the use of school-based 
mathematics  in  practical  situations. This  duality  makes  mathematical  modelling 
typically an activity that is affected by the world beyond the educational institu-
tions. For example, teachers and students can have experiences with mathematical 
modelling  in  informal  fields,  such  as  at  home  or  through  newspape rs  and  other 
